{"idx": 0, "uid": "mimic-safe-21133938-Magnesiumsulfate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 19 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of furosemide (loop diuretic) create a high-risk scenario for magnesium toxicity. Magnesium sulfate is primarily eliminated renally; with acute kidney failure and ongoing diuretic therapy, clearance is markedly reduced, leading to accumulation and potential cardiac arrhythmias, neuromuscular depression, and respiratory depression. Additionally, the patient's mixed acid-base disorder and hypoxia further compromise magnesium homeostasis, amplifying toxicity risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 1, "uid": "mimic-age-24656677-Paroxetine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (81 years) combined with moderate renal impairment (eGFR 41 mL/min/1.73m2) and uncontrolled diabetic ketoacidosis. Paroxetine, a potent CYP2D6 inhibitor, is prescribed at 40 mg daily - the maximum dose - in a patient already on multiple interacting medications (insulin, omeprazole, ranitidine, heparin, buspirone, atorvastatin, glucagon, cyanocobalamin). This constellation creates a high-risk scenario for drug-drug interactions, altered pharmacokinetics, and adverse events (e.g., hyponatremia, serotonin syndrome, bleeding, and worsened glycemic control). The patient's acute kidney failure further reduces clearance of paroxetine and its active metabolites, compounding toxicity risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 2, "uid": "mimic-ddi-25563031-Zolpidem", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and electrolyte-altering medications creates a hazard when combined with zolpidem. Specifically, the co-administration of oxycodone (opioid), lorazepam (benzodiazepine), epinephrine (sympathomimetic), and magnesium sulfate (electrolyte) with zolpidem elevates the risk of excessive sedation, respiratory depression, and cardiac arrhythmia. Additionally, the patient's documented anaphylactic reaction history introduces a potential hypersensitivity concern, even though zolpidem is not a common allergen.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 3, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29462354-Fluconazole", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's active acute myeloid leukemia (without remission) and concurrent use of multiple potent CYP3A4 and CYP2C9 substrates (oxycodone, dexamethasone, lorazepam, ciprofloxacin, acyclovir, daunorubicin, cytarabine) create a high-risk pharmacokinetic environment. Fluconazole, a strong CYP3A4 and CYP2C9 inhibitor, will elevate the plasma concentrations of these drugs, increasing the likelihood of dose-dependent toxicity (e.g., respiratory depression, excessive sedation, hepatotoxicity, or myelosuppression). Additionally, the patient's pancytopenia (platelets 28 K/uL) and ongoing infections (bacteremia, viral pneumonia) further amplify the hazard of adverse drug reactions and superinfection.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 4, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28335091-Hydrocodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of gabapentin (300 mg TID) and hydrocodone (1 tab BID) creates a high-risk drug-drug interaction. Gabapentin is a gabapentinoid that potentiates opioid effects, increasing the likelihood of respiratory depression, sedation, and hypotension. Additionally, the patient's NSTEMI status, ongoing use of ibuprofen (NSAID), and multiple cardiovascular medications (furosemide, losartan, metoprolol, pantoprazole) further elevate bleeding and hemodynamic instability risks. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions, rendering the hydrocodone order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 5, "uid": "mimic-age-21255400-Metoclopramide", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's concurrent use of metoclopramide with morphine, oxycodone, and nitroglycerin. Metoclopramide is a dopamine D2 receptor antagonist that can blunt the central nervous system depressant effects of opioids, potentially masking signs of opioid toxicity (e.g., respiratory depression, sedation). Additionally, metoclopramide's prokinetic action may alter gastric emptying, increasing the risk of aspiration if the patient is already sedated from opioid analgesia. The patient's age (78 years) further compounds vulnerability to CNS depression and falls.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 6, "uid": "mimic-safe-28723315-Pravastatin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### 1. CLINICAL ASSESSMENT\\nThe patient's concurrent use of lisinopril (ACE inhibitor), acetylsalicylic acid (ASA), and clopidogrel (P2Y12 inhibitor) creates a high-risk triple-therapy regimen. Adding pravastatin introduces a fourth antithrombotic agent, compounding bleeding hazard.\\n\\n### 2. PHARMACOLOGICAL BASIS\\nCombining an ACE inhibitor with dual antiplatelet therapy (ASA + clopidogrel) is standard for secondary prevention in coronary artery disease. However, statins (pravastatin) do not directly affect platelet aggregation or hemostasis, but their addition to this regimen does not mitigate the inherent bleeding risk; rather, it may slightly increase it due to additive pharmacodynamic effects. The combination is not contraindicated, but the patient's profile already places him at elevated bleeding risk, and the addition of any new drug should be carefully justified.\\n\\n### 3. PATIENT-SPECIFIC ANALYSIS\\nGeneral safety assumptions for pravastatin (e.g., low bleeding risk, no major drug interactions) are overridden by the patient's specific polypharmacy. The presence of three established antithrombotic agents means any new drug, even with a low intrinsic bleeding risk, could push the patient into a clinically significant bleeding event. Thus, the order is deemed unsafe.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 7, "uid": "mimic-ddi-20214994-Haloperidol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 47) and multiple myeloma with unspecified protein-calorie malnutrition create a high-risk milieu for haloperidol accumulation. The concurrent use of Heparin, Norepinephrine, and Fentanyl further elevates the risk of QT prolongation and cardiac arrhythmia. The patient's advanced age (75 years) and low platelet count (49 K/uL) compound the hazard of bleeding and neurotoxicity. These overlapping factors override any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 8, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24997044-Amiodarone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple drugs with known QT-prolonging or proarrhythmic properties - including warfarin, ciprofloxacin, metoclopramide, phenylephrine, propofol, cefazolin, neostigmine, oxycodone, morphine, and glycopyrronium - creates a high-risk pharmacodynamic interaction with amiodarone. Additionally, the patient's paroxysmal ventricular tachycardia and atrial fibrillation are pre-existing cardiac conditions that amplify the arrhythmogenic potential of amiodarone. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 9, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27494880-Gabapentin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and anticoagulant/antiplatelet agents (fluoxetine, ziprasidone, hydrocodone, valproic acid, trazodone) with gabapentin creates a high risk of additive sedation, respiratory depression, and bleeding. Additionally, the patient's obesity (BMI 36.7) and advanced age (61 years) further amplify these risks. The presence of obstructive sleep apnea compounds the sedation hazard, and the extensive polypharmacy list (17 concurrent medications) indicates a complex, high-risk pharmacotherapy environment.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 10, "uid": "mimic-safe-23473524-Potassiumchloride", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **acute-on-chronic combined systolic and diastolic heart failure** (cardiac impairment). This condition creates a high-risk substrate for hyperkalemia, especially when administering potassium chloride intravenously. The ordered dose of 60 mEq PO is excessive for a patient with compromised cardiac function and concurrent renal impairment (eGFR 99 mL/min/1.73m2 is normal, but the cardiac vulnerability overrides this). Additionally, the patient is on **insulin human** and **famotidine**, which can alter potassium homeostasis and reduce renal potassium excretion, further amplifying the risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
