{"idx": 12, "uid": "mimic-safe-27996267-Febuxostat", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 30 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent use of ciprofloxacin, a potent inhibitor of CYP2C9, which is the major metabolic pathway for febuxostat. This creates a high-risk drug-drug interaction and a dose-dependent toxicity concern due to impaired clearance.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 13, "uid": "mimic-safe-28166872-Ampicillin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - fentanyl, midazolam, propofol, lorazepam, morphine, hydralazine, and phenylephrine - create a high-risk pharmacodynamic interaction with ampicillin. The primary hazard is additive CNS depression and respiratory depression. Additionally, the patient's acute respiratory failure and methicillin-resistant pneumonia (MRSA) necessitate a narrow-spectrum antibiotic, but ampicillin is not effective against MRSA, making the choice clinically inappropriate.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 14, "uid": "mimic-safe-28258130-Glucagon", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 30 mL/min/1.73m2) and moderate hepatic impairment (alcoholic cirrhosis, ALT 70/AST 139) create a dual hazard. Glucagon is contraindicated in acute renal failure because it promotes calcium mobilization and can precipitate hypocalcemia, which is particularly dangerous in a patient already receiving intravenous calcium gluconate and potassium chloride. Additionally, glucagon's hepatotoxic potential is amplified in cirrhosis, where hepatic clearance is reduced. The concurrent use of norepinephrine and vasopressin further elevates the risk of electrolyte and hemodynamic instability. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 15, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27553957-Irbesartan", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 35 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent use of multiple nephrotoxic and anticoagulant/antiplatelet agents. Irbesartan, an angiotensin II receptor blocker, is itself renally cleared and can further impair renal function in the setting of pre-existing acute kidney injury. The patient's polypharmacy includes warfarin, aspirin, oxycodone, metoprolol, labetalol, and spironolactone, each of which carries a known risk of nephrotoxicity or renal hemodynamic compromise. The addition of irbesartan to this already compromised renal system creates a synergistic risk of acute kidney injury progression, electrolyte disturbances (hyperkalemia), and bleeding due to additive antiplatelet/anticoagulant effects. The patient's atrial fibrillation and congestive heart failure further complicate management, but the immediate safety concern is the renal and drug-interaction hazard.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 16, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22390287-Diltiazem", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent use of diltiazem with oxycodone, hydromorphone, citalopram, and metoprolol. Diltiazem is a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker that depresses the central nervous system and can potentiate the sedative and respiratory-depressant effects of opioids. Additionally, diltiazem inhibits CYP3A4, the primary enzyme responsible for oxycodone metabolism, potentially elevating its plasma concentration and increasing the risk of opioid toxicity. Citalopram, a weak CYP2C19 inhibitor, may also contribute to additive CNS depression when combined with diltiazem. Metoprolol, a beta-blocker, adds further bradycardic and hypotensive effects, compounding the risk of bradycardia and hypotension. The patient's advanced age (75 years) and obesity (BMI 31.5) further reduce drug clearance and increase sensitivity to these interactions. The ordered dose of diltiazem 120 mg once daily is standard for hypertension, but the patient's polypharmacy and age render this dose potentially excessive, especially given the need for dose reduction in elderly patients with renal impairment (though eGFR is normal). Thus, the combination of diltiazem with multiple CNS-depressant and cardiovascular agents, along with age-related pharmacokinetic changes, makes this order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 17, "uid": "mimic-age-21476294-Cyclobenzaprine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - oxycodone, lorazepam, etoposide, dexamethasone, and cyclophosphamide (implied from mitoxantrone and cytarabine) - create a high-risk polypharmacy profile. Cyclobenzaprine, a centrally acting muscle relaxant with sedative properties, is contraindicated in this context due to additive CNS depression and potential for respiratory depression. The patient's neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, and ongoing chemotherapy further amplify vulnerability to infection and bleeding, making any additional sedative agent unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 18, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24980601-Furosemide", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent use of **sucralfate** (a cationic bismuth-based antacid) with **furosemide**. Sucralfate can bind to furosemide in the gastrointestinal tract, markedly reducing its oral bioavailability and efficacy. Additionally, the patient's **conversion disorder with mixed symptom presentation** - a psychosomatic condition - creates a scenario where furosemide's diuretic and electrolyte-altering effects could be misattributed to the disorder, potentially complicating clinical interpretation and management. The patient's **severe obesity (BMI 37.3)** further amplifies the risk of volume depletion and electrolyte disturbances, especially given his existing **hypoglycemia without coma** and **diabetic polyneuropathy**.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 19, "uid": "mimic-renal-29842315-Gabapentin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk", "Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 6 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (89 years). Gabapentin is renally eliminated; in this setting, accumulation and neurotoxicity are inevitable. Additionally, the patient's respiratory failure and concurrent methadone therapy create a high risk of additive respiratory depression.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 20, "uid": "mimic-safe-26529390-Hydroxyurea", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with the ordered hydroxyurea dose of 500 mg once daily. Hydroxyurea is predominantly eliminated renally; in acute renal impairment, clearance is reduced, leading to drug accumulation and elevated risk of myelosuppression (especially thrombocytopenia) and bone marrow suppression. Additionally, the patient's splenic infarction (a condition that can impair splenic function and platelet sequestration) further heightens the risk of bleeding, as evidenced by the current platelet count of 28 \u00d7 10^3/\u03bcL. The concurrent use of multiple medications (e.g., tramadol, escitalopram, lidocaine) adds complexity, but the renal and splenic factors are the decisive triggers.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 21, "uid": "mimic-safe-21133938-Dopamine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 20 mL/min/1.73m2) and acute respiratory failure with hypoxia create a dual hazard. Dopamine is primarily eliminated renally; in severe renal failure, its metabolites and active fraction accumulate, risking hyperkalemia, pulmonary edema, and arrhythmias. Additionally, dopamine's alpha-adrenergic effects can precipitate or worsen pulmonary congestion in a patient already in acute respiratory failure. The concurrent hyperkalemia (6.8 mEq/L) further elevates the risk of cardiac arrest if dopamine-induced renal impairment worsens.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 22, "uid": "mimic-safe-27708593-Cyanocobalamin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### 1. CLINICAL ASSESSMENT\\nThe patient's concurrent use of morphine (15 mg PO/NG daily) and diazepam (2.5-5 mg PO/NG) creates a high-risk CNS depressant combination. Cyanocobalamin (vitamin B12) is not a depressant, but the patient's baseline respiratory reserve is already compromised by advanced age (66 years) and the presence of neurogenic claudication (which may indicate spinal cord vulnerability). The additive sedative effect of morphine and diazepam can suppress respiratory drive, and the patient's age further reduces compensatory mechanisms. This combination overrides any general safety assumptions about B12 supplementation.\\n\\n### 2.\\n\\n### 3.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 23, "uid": "mimic-age-27327816-Zolpidem", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent use of warfarin (2 mg daily) and zolpidem (5 mg). Warfarin is a narrow-therapeutic-index anticoagulant, and zolpidem is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor. This interaction can significantly elevate warfarin plasma concentrations, increasing the risk of major bleeding. Additionally, the patient's atrial fibrillation, for which warfarin is indicated, further amplifies the bleeding hazard. The presence of multiple other interacting drugs (e.g., oxycodone, lisinopril, omeprazole) compounds the risk, but the warfarin-zolpidem interaction is the decisive factor.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
