{"idx": 36, "uid": "mimic-safe-26703331-Lidocaine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute respiratory failure with hypoxia (eGFR 60, moderate renal impairment) combined with a current medication list that includes ciprofloxacin (a moderate CYP1A2 inhibitor) and hydromorphone (a potent opioid). Lidocaine is primarily metabolized by CYP1A2 and CYP3A4; co-administration with ciprofloxacin elevates lidocaine exposure, while concurrent opioid use synergistically depresses the respiratory drive, directly threatening airway patency in a patient already in hypoxic respiratory failure.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 37, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22539296-Acetylsalicylicacid", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 32 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent warfarin therapy. Adding acetylsalicylic acid (ASA) creates a dual antithrombotic regimen that amplifies bleeding risk, especially in the setting of renal impairment where hemostatic clearance is already compromised.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 38, "uid": "mimic-age-29279905-Hydroxyzine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 20 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (70 years) create a high hazard for hydroxyzine accumulation. Additionally, the patient is currently receiving norepinephrine for septic shock, and hydroxyzine possesses anticholinergic properties that can antagonize the pressor effects of alpha-adrenergic agonists, risking hypotension and hemodynamic instability. These two interacting factors - renal impairment and concurrent vasoactive drug - override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 39, "uid": "mimic-safe-24159665-Omeprazole", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent administration of **sucralfate** (1 gm, 4 times/day PO/NG) and **omeprazole** (20 mg once daily PO). Sucralfate is a cationic, non-absorbable polymer that binds to cations in the GI lumen, including the proton-pump inhibitor omeprazole. This binding reduces omeprazole absorption by up to 90%, rendering the prescribed dose ineffective. Additionally, omeprazole is a CYP2C19 substrate; sucralfate may also inhibit CYP2C19, further compromising omeprazole metabolism. The patient's neoplasm and polypharmacy amplify the risk of occult bleeding (due to aspirin and heparin) and infection (due to neoplasm and immunosuppressive agents), but the immediate safety issue is the drug-drug interaction.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 40, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24745425-Glycopyrronium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple drugs with known QT-prolonging, proarrhythmic, or hypotensive properties - including fluoxetine, fentanyl, oxycodone, meperidine, epinephrine, phenylephrine, potassium chloride, and others - creates a high-risk pharmacodynamic milieu. Glycopyrronium, a long-acting anticholinergic, can further impair cardiac conduction and increase heart rate, synergistically amplifying the QT-interval prolongation and arrhythmia potential. The patient's advanced age (72 years) and moderate renal impairment (eGFR 37 mL/min) further reduce drug clearance and increase sensitivity. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 41, "uid": "mimic-ddi-23251352-Glycopyrronium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's chronic total occlusion of a coronary artery and the concurrent use of multiple cardiodepressant agents (morphine, oxycodone, nitroglycerin, metoclopramide, neostigmine, propofol, calcium gluconate, potassium chloride, magnesium sulfate). Glycopyrronium, a non-selective anticholinergic, can cause tachycardia and increased heart rate, which directly antagonizes the negative chronotropic and inotropic effects of these drugs. In a patient with coronary atherosclerosis and a chronic total occlusion, any increase in heart rate or myocardial oxygen demand can precipitate ischemia, arrhythmia, or infarction. The patient's age (66 years) further amplifies the risk of adverse cardiac events. Thus, the combination of glycopyrronium with this polypharmacy and underlying coronary pathology renders the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 42, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29839885-Olanzapine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's mechanical complication of an automatic implantable cardiac defibrillator (ICD) combined with chronic systolic heart failure. Olanzapine, an atypical antipsychotic, carries a known risk of QT-interval prolongation and can exacerbate cardiac conduction abnormalities, which is particularly dangerous in a patient with an ICD and underlying heart failure. Additionally, the patient's moderate renal impairment (eGFR 42 mL/min) may reduce clearance of olanzapine metabolites, further elevating systemic exposure and cardiac risk. The concurrent use of multiple QT-prolonging or proarrhythmic drugs (e.g., oxycodone, topiramate, cephalexin, alprazolam) compounds this danger, creating a high-risk profile for torsades de pointes or ICD activation.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 43, "uid": "mimic-safe-23831430-Lidocaine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - fentanyl, calcium gluconate, potassium phosphate, potassium chloride, magnesium sulfate, and insulin - collectively create a high-risk milieu for lidocaine toxicity. The primary hazard is the drug-drug interaction that predisposes to lidocaine-induced arrhythmia, especially in the setting of septicemia and acute kidney failure.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 44, "uid": "mimic-ddi-23514107-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's current use of hydromorphone (0.25 mg IV daily) - an opioid analgesic - combined with the proposed oxycodone. Both agents are mu-opioid agonists, creating a pharmacodynamic drug-drug interaction that elevates the risk of opioid-induced respiratory depression, sedation, and potential overdose. Additionally, the patient's intestinal adhesions with obstruction (postinfection) further heightens the danger: opioids can cause histamine release and smooth muscle relaxation, which may exacerbate bowel obstruction or trigger paralytic ileus, a life-threatening complication in this setting.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 45, "uid": "mimic-safe-22081550-Cefepime", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - midazolam, fentanyl, propofol, norepinephrine, valproic acid, citalopram, and vancomycin - create a high-risk pharmacodynamic profile. Cefepime, a cephalosporin, can potentiate neuromuscular blockade when co-administered with neuromuscular blocking agents (e.g., fentanyl, propofol) and may also cause additive respiratory depression with opioids. Additionally, cefepime carries a known risk of seizures, which is amplified by the patient's concurrent use of valproic acid (an anticonvulsant) and citalopram (a serotonergic antidepressant with a low but documented seizure threshold effect). The presence of acute respiratory failure further elevates the hazard of respiratory depression. These overlapping mechanisms override any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 46, "uid": "mimic-ddi-20214994-Tramadol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's concurrent use of multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants - metoprolol, diltiazem, lorazepam, and ciprofloxacin - combined with the proposed tramadol. This polypharmacy creates a high risk of additive sedation, respiratory depression, and cardiovascular instability. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (75 years) and obesity (BMI 32.7) further amplify these risks.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 47, "uid": "mimic-age-29842315-Hyoscyamine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 9 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (89 years) create a high-risk pharmacokinetic environment for hyoscyamine. The drug's active metabolites accumulate in renal failure, and elderly patients have reduced clearance and increased sensitivity to anticholinergic effects. Additionally, the patient is on multiple CNS-active medications (methadone, hydromorphone, lorazepam, glycopyrronium) that synergistically depress the central nervous system, raising the risk of severe sedation, respiratory depression, and cardiac arrhythmias. The presence of NSTEMI, cardiac arrest history, and diastolic heart failure further amplifies the danger of hyoscyamine-induced tachycardia, arrhythmia, and electrolyte destabilization (e.g., hyperkalemia from concurrent potassium 5.4 mEq/L). These overlapping vulnerabilities override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
