{"idx": 66, "uid": "mimic-age-21255400-Clonazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of lorazepam (0.5-2 mg IV) and the newly ordered clonazepam (0.5 mg PO/NG). Both are benzodiazepines that potentiate GABA-A receptor activity, leading to additive CNS depression. The patient's age (78 years) further amplifies this risk, as elderly patients are more sensitive to benzodiazepine effects and have a higher incidence of falls, sedation, and respiratory depression. Additionally, the patient's coronary atherosclerosis and intermediate coronary syndrome create a vulnerable cardiac substrate, and excessive sedation could mask or worsen cardiac instability.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 67, "uid": "mimic-safe-21791856-Glucagon", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 14 mL/min/1.73m2) and severe respiratory compromise (COPD exacerbation with hypoxia). Glucagon is contraindicated in acute kidney injury because it can precipitate hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis, which are particularly dangerous in renal failure. Additionally, glucagon may cause bronchospasm or worsen respiratory failure in patients with COPD, especially those on concurrent bronchodilators (ipratropium) and corticosteroids (methylprednisolone). The patient's polypharmacy further elevates risk, as drug interactions (e.g., with insulin, diuretics, or anticoagulants) are not well-characterized and could compound adverse effects.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 68, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28829452-Levofloxacin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute respiratory failure with hypoxia is the critical hazard. Levofloxacin, a fluoroquinolone, carries a known risk of exacerbating respiratory depression, especially in patients with pre-existing respiratory compromise. The concurrent use of multiple CNS depressants (alprazolam, morphine, gabapentin, buspirone, escitalopram) further amplifies the risk of additive sedation and respiratory depression. The patient's compromised respiratory status overrides any general safety assumptions, making the ordered levofloxacin unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 69, "uid": "mimic-safe-23983182-Metronidazole", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's advanced age (80 years), concurrent use of heparin (anticoagulant), and the addition of metronidazole, which can independently prolong the international normalized ratio (INR). This combination elevates the risk of bleeding complications. Additionally, the patient's chronic heart failure and coronary atherosclerosis create a vulnerable hemodynamic state that may be exacerbated by any prothrombotic or prohemorrhagic drug interaction.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 70, "uid": "mimic-safe-23983182-Meropenem", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's advanced age (80 years), concurrent use of multiple nephrotoxic agents (potassium chloride, potassium phosphate, alteplase, magnesium sulfate), and a mildly reduced eGFR of 76 mL/min/1.73m2. Meropenem, while generally safe, carries a known risk of acute kidney injury, especially in elderly patients with pre-existing renal impairment and polypharmacy. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 71, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27738145-Sertraline", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - fentanyl, propofol, hydromorphone, levofloxacin, and octreotide - all carry a known risk of QT prolongation. Adding sertraline, a weak QT-prolonging SSRI, creates a cumulative hazard for torsades de pointes. Additionally, the patient's acute respiratory failure and ventilator-associated pneumonia increase the risk of serotonin syndrome from sertraline's serotonergic activity. The combination of multiple CNS depressants with sertraline further elevates the risk of excessive sedation and respiratory depression.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 72, "uid": "mimic-ddi-20755971-Ciprofloxacin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent use of ciprofloxacin with insulin human and glucagon in a patient with spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, acute respiratory failure, and paroxysmal ventricular tachycardia. Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 and CYP2C9, reducing insulin clearance and increasing hypoglycemic risk. It also potentiates the hypoglycemic effect of exogenous glucagon, creating a dangerous additive effect. Additionally, ciprofloxacin prolongs the QT interval, which is especially concerning in a patient with pre-existing cardiac impairment (paroxysmal ventricular tachycardia) and acute respiratory failure, where electrolyte disturbances and hemodynamic instability are common. The patient's cachexia and oliguria/anuria further compromise renal and metabolic homeostasis, amplifying the risk of hypoglycemia and arrhythmia.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 73, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28003918-Ondansetron", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's moderate renal impairment (eGFR 56 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent use of oxycodone and hydromorphone - both opioid analgesics - and the addition of ondansetron, a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist with serotonergic activity. This constellation creates a high-risk scenario for additive CNS depression and serotonin syndrome.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 74, "uid": "mimic-age-29276678-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute respiratory failure with hypoxia, which directly contraindicates lorazepam. This condition is exacerbated by concurrent use of multiple CNS-depressant agents (oxycodone, hydromorphone, voriconazole, ondansetron, acyclovir, cefepime, vancomycin, allopurinol, tamoxifen). Lorazepam, a benzodiazepine, further depresses the central nervous system and respiratory drive, creating a synergistic risk of severe respiratory depression, hypoxemia, and potential respiratory arrest. Additionally, the patient's moderate renal impairment (eGFR 55) and hepatic enzyme elevation (ALT 55, AST 94) may alter lorazepam clearance, but the overriding respiratory risk makes the drug unsafe.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 75, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24997044-Ciprofloxacin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient is currently on Warfarin (1 dose, once daily, PO/NG) for atrial fibrillation and paroxysmal ventricular tachycardia. The addition of Ciprofloxacin, a fluoroquinolone antibiotic, creates a well-documented drug-drug interaction that elevates the risk of major bleeding. Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 and CYP2C9, the primary enzymes responsible for Warfarin metabolism, leading to increased Warfarin exposure and prolonged INR. This interaction is clinically significant and contraindicated unless close INR monitoring and dose adjustment are implemented, which are not indicated in this scenario.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 76, "uid": "mimic-ddi-26013492-Acetylsalicylicacid", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 97 mL/min/1.73m2) is not a true renal impairment; however, the presence of multiple interacting medications (clopidogrel, rivaroxaban, warfarin, valproic acid, metoprolol, hydrocodone, lorazepam, trazodone, ondansetron, gabapentin, senna, polyethylene glycol) combined with the addition of acetylsalicylic acid creates a high-risk drug-drug interaction profile. The patient's chronic conditions (heart failure, atrial fibrillation, diabetes, nephropathy) further amplify bleeding and cardiovascular risks. The ordered ASA dose (81 mg) is standard, but the context of concurrent anticoagulants and antiplatelets, plus the potential for additive nephrotoxicity from polyethylene glycol and senna, renders the regimen unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 77, "uid": "mimic-age-27708593-Diazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of morphine (15 mg PO/NG) and diazepam (2.5 mg PO/NG). Morphine is a mu-opioid agonist, while diazepam is a benzodiazepine that potentiates GABA-A receptor activity. The combination produces additive CNS depression, increasing the risk of excessive sedation, respiratory depression, and potential respiratory arrest. This interaction is well-documented and contraindicated in many clinical guidelines unless closely monitored.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
