{"idx": 86, "uid": "mimic-safe-23473524-Heparin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (81 years) combined with septicemia, acute respiratory failure, and acute-on-chronic heart failure. These conditions create a high-risk milieu for heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) and excessive bleeding. The patient is already on multiple medications (e.g., norepinephrine, propofol, cefepime, vancomycin, ciprofloxacin) that can independently affect coagulation or mask bleeding signs. The ordered heparin dose (5000 units SC three times daily) is standard for prophylaxis, but in this vulnerable profile the risk of HIT and hemorrhage outweighs any potential benefit, rendering the order unsafe.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 87, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27112038-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent administration of oxycodone with ciprofloxacin, metronidazole, and hydromorphone - all of which are potent CYP3A4 and/or CYP2C19 inhibitors. This creates a high-risk drug-drug interaction that can severely elevate oxycodone plasma concentrations, leading to opioid toxicity.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 88, "uid": "mimic-age-27089790-Metoclopramide", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's advanced age (78 years), concurrent use of multiple high-risk medications (e.g., morphine, oxycodone, propofol, phenylephrine, nitroglycerin, cefazolin, insulin, potassium chloride, magnesium sulfate, neostigmine, glycopyrronium, atorvastatin, acetylsalicylic acid), and pre-existing coronary atherosclerosis with an eGFR of 69 mL/min/1.73m2 create a multifactorial hazard. The addition of metoclopramide - a dopamine D2-receptor antagonist with known proarrhythmic, proconvulsant, and extrapyramidal effects - to this complex regimen amplifies the risk of QT-interval prolongation, torsades de pointes, and drug-drug interactions (e.g., additive CNS depression, altered metabolism of other agents). The patient's profile overrides any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 89, "uid": "mimic-safe-20093566-Octreotide", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 5 mL/min/1.73m2) and alcoholic hepatic failure without coma. Octreotide is primarily eliminated renally, and its clearance is severely reduced in this setting, leading to drug accumulation and potential toxicity. Additionally, the hepatic impairment may alter first-pass metabolism, further elevating systemic exposure. The combination of these two impairments creates a dual-mechanism risk that overrides any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 90, "uid": "mimic-age-22130791-Diphenhydramine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 23 mL/min/1.73m2, acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis) and advanced age (88 years). Diphenhydramine is predominantly eliminated renally; in this setting, accumulation of the drug and its active metabolites (e.g., methoxyphenidine) markedly increases the risk of central nervous system toxicity (confusion, hallucinations, seizures) and anticholinergic adverse effects (constipation, urinary retention, tachycardia). Additionally, diphenhydramine can exacerbate hypotension and worsen hypovolemic shock, which is already present. The patient's melena and thrombocytopenia further heighten the risk of gastrointestinal bleeding, and the concurrent use of pantoprazole (IV) and diphenhydramine may potentiate bleeding via altered platelet function or coagulation. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
