{"idx": 116, "uid": "mimic-renal-29974575-Pregabalin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 29 mL/min/1.73 m2) combined with advanced age (72 years). Pregabalin is predominantly eliminated unchanged by the kidneys; in this setting, accumulation of the drug and its active metabolite (Mephamine) leads to a high risk of dose-dependent adverse effects, including excessive sedation, dizziness, and cognitive impairment. Additionally, the patient's concurrent use of multiple CNS-active agents (clonazepam, mirtazapine, salbutamol, insulin, glucagon, senna, etc.) creates a complex pharmacodynamic profile that further amplifies the risk of respiratory depression, falls, and altered mental status. The presence of septic shock and acute respiratory failure also heightens vulnerability to any depressant effect on the central nervous system or respiratory drive.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 117, "uid": "mimic-safe-21791856-Febuxostat", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 14 mL/min/1.73m2) and severe respiratory compromise (COPD exacerbation with hypoxia). Febuxostat, a xanthine oxidase inhibitor, carries a boxed warning for acute febrile reactions and can precipitate acute kidney injury. In a patient with already critically low renal function, the drug's metabolite (oxipurinol) accumulates, elevating the risk of nephrotoxicity. Additionally, the patient's respiratory failure creates a high baseline risk of hypoxemia, which could be exacerbated by febrile reactions or drug-induced adverse effects. The concurrent use of multiple medications (e.g., metolazone, torasemide, carvedilol, insulin, and others) further complicates safety, but the renal and respiratory contraindications are decisive.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 118, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27327816-Tramadol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of warfarin (2 mg daily) and sotalol (120 mg twice daily) creates a dual hazard when tramadol is added. Warfarin is a narrow-therapeutic-index anticoagulant, and sotalol is a class III antiarrhythmic with QT-prolonging potential. Tramadol, a serotonergic opioid, can further elevate INR via CYP2C19 inhibition and may potentiate QT prolongation through additive effects on cardiac repolarization. The patient's atrial fibrillation and normal renal function do not mitigate these interactions; rather, the combination of three interacting agents (warfarin, sotalol, tramadol) overrides any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
