{"idx": 139, "uid": "mimic-safe-20192635-Cyanocobalamin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 35 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with her current use of insulin human and multiple interacting medications. Cyanocobalamin, while generally safe, can mask the neurological signs of vitamin B12 deficiency, which may be exacerbated by insulin-induced hypoglycemia and other drug interactions. Additionally, the renal impairment reduces clearance of certain agents, increasing the risk of toxicity from the concurrent medications.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
