{"idx": 144, "uid": "mimic-ddi-21027282-Furosemide", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severely reduced eGFR (18 mL/min/1.73m2) and acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis create a critical hazard for furosemide. Furosemide is a loop diuretic that can precipitate acute kidney injury, electrolyte imbalances (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hyperuricemia), and worsen pre-existing renal function. Additionally, the patient's concurrent use of fentanyl, norepinephrine, and propofol for hemodynamic support, combined with the diuretic's potential to cause hypovolemia and hypotension, elevates the risk of hemodynamic instability. The presence of paroxysmal ventricular tachycardia and atrial fibrillation further complicates safety, as diuretic-induced hypovolemia can trigger arrhythmias. The ordered dose (40 mg IV) is standard but the patient's profile overrides general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
