{"idx": 150, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29483621-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple potent CYP3A4 and CYP2D6 inhibitors (diltiazem, metoprolol, nitroglycerin, senna, polyethylene glycol, lidocaine, magnesium sulfate, potassium chloride) creates a high-risk drug-drug interaction with oxycodone, which is metabolized by these pathways. Additionally, the patient's atrial fibrillation, hypotension, and advanced age amplify the risk of opioid-induced respiratory depression and cardiovascular instability. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 151, "uid": "mimic-ddi-26529390-Escitalopram", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent use of tramadol (50 mg PO). Escitalopram, a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor, and tramadol, a serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor with serotonergic activity, create a high-risk scenario for serotonin syndrome. Additionally, the patient's renal impairment reduces clearance of both agents, further amplifying the risk. The presence of acute respiratory failure with hypoxia and congestive heart failure adds vulnerability to any adverse event.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 152, "uid": "mimic-age-20192635-Paroxetine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's moderate renal impairment (eGFR 35 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with the ordered paroxetine dose of 40 mg once daily. Paroxetine is extensively metabolized by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, but its elimination half-life is prolonged in renal dysfunction, increasing the risk of serotonin syndrome and QT prolongation. Additionally, the patient's poisoning by insulins and antidiabetic agents (including insulin human) creates a high baseline risk of hypoglycemia, which paroxetine can exacerbate via its serotonergic and sympathomimetic effects. The concurrent use of multiple antihypertensives (hydrochlorothiazide, amlodipine, losartan) and the presence of hypothermia further compound the danger of cardiovascular instability. The patient's advanced age (80 years) and polypharmacy further reduce safety margins.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 153, "uid": "mimic-safe-24420677-Lidocaine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 32 mL/min/1.73m2, stage 4 CKD) combined with advanced age (87 years) and acute-on-chronic systolic heart failure. Lidocaine is a class IB antiarrhythmic that undergoes hepatic metabolism and is renally eliminated as active metabolites. In this setting, accumulation of lidocaine and its metabolites can precipitate lidocaine toxicity (proarrhythmia, seizures, cardiovascular collapse). Additionally, the patient is receiving norepinephrine (vasopressor) for hypovolemic shock, which may further compromise coronary perfusion and increase arrhythmia risk. The proposed intravenous route (5 mL SC) is not standard for lidocaine; typical administration is intravenous push or infusion, and subcutaneous delivery is not recommended due to unpredictable absorption and potential for local anesthetic toxicity. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 154, "uid": "mimic-renal-25172300-Spironolactone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk", "Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 27 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent use of loop diuretics (torasemide 40 mg BID) and an ACE inhibitor (irbesartan 150 mg daily). Adding spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic, creates a high-risk scenario for hyperkalemia. Additionally, the patient's atrial fibrillation, congestive heart failure, and chronic diastolic heart failure further amplify the danger of electrolyte disturbances and cardiac arrhythmias. The high body weight (100.7 kg) does not mitigate these risks; rather, it may increase volume overload and diuretic sensitivity.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 155, "uid": "mimic-safe-23473524-Ceftriaxone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes **Potassium chloride (60 mEq, once daily, PO)** and **Acetylsalicylic acid (1 CAP, once daily, PO)**. The ordered **Ceftriaxone 1 gm IV** creates a dual hazard: (1) a drug-drug interaction that can elevate serum potassium, and (2) a pharmacodynamic interaction that may potentiate gastrointestinal bleeding.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 156, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28477357-Tamoxifen", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute alcoholic hepatitis (hepatic impairment) and concurrent duloxetine therapy create the primary hazards. Tamoxifen is extensively metabolized by CYP2D6 and CYP3A4; duloxetine is a moderate CYP2D6 inhibitor. In the setting of acute liver injury, tamoxifen clearance is further reduced, elevating systemic exposure and toxicity risk. Additionally, nicotine (a CYP1A2 substrate) may alter tamoxifen metabolism, but the dominant concern is the drug-drug interaction with duloxetine.\\n\\n2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 157, "uid": "mimic-safe-20199380-Ranitidine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes multiple agents with known pharmacokinetic interactions with ranitidine. Specifically, ranitidine inhibits CYP2C9 and CYP3A4, which are critical for the metabolism of oxycodone (CYP3A4) and hydromorphone (CYP2C9). This co-administration can elevate opioid concentrations, increasing the risk of respiratory depression, sedation, and other opioid-related adverse effects. Additionally, ranitidine may reduce the absorption of levothyroxine, potentially leading to suboptimal thyroid hormone replacement. The patient's obesity (BMI 34.8) further amplifies the risk of opioid-induced respiratory compromise, as fat mass can alter drug distribution and clearance. These combined factors override any general safety assumptions, rendering the ranitidine order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 158, "uid": "mimic-renal-20093566-Baclofen", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 4 mL/min/1.73m2) and alcoholic hepatic failure without coma. Baclofen is predominantly renally eliminated; in severe renal impairment, clearance is markedly reduced, leading to drug accumulation and risk of excessive CNS depression. Additionally, baclofen is hepatically metabolized, and hepatic failure can further impair clearance, compounding the risk. The patient is already on lorazepam (a CNS depressant) and midodrine (which may cause orthostatic hypotension). The combination of baclofen with these agents creates additive sedation and potential cardiovascular instability. The high body weight (96.9 kg, BMI 39.1) does not mitigate these risks; rather, it underscores the need for dose reduction or avoidance in this vulnerable profile.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 159, "uid": "mimic-age-24225421-Paroxetine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **moderately reduced renal function (eGFR 32 mL/min/1.73m2)** combined with **advanced age (81 years)**, which together create a high risk of paroxetine accumulation and adverse events. Additionally, the patient is currently on **buspirone**, a serotonergic agent, and **furosemide**, which can potentiate paroxetine's anticholinergic and QT-prolonging effects. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions for paroxetine use.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 160, "uid": "mimic-ddi-20611640-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of methadone (10 mg 3 times daily) and the proposed oxycodone (5-10 mg) creates a critical drug-drug interaction hazard. Both agents are mu-opioid agonists, and co-administration produces additive central nervous system and respiratory depression. Additionally, the patient's history of intracerebral hemorrhage, cerebral edema, and hemiplegia from a brain tumor further elevates the risk of opioid-induced respiratory compromise, sedation, and altered mental status, which could worsen neurological outcomes. The presence of multiple other CNS-active medications (e.g., dexamethasone, levetiracetam, lidocaine) compounds this vulnerability.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 161, "uid": "mimic-safe-28861356-Pantoprazole", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's advanced age (80 years), moderate renal impairment (eGFR 56), and concurrent use of multiple interacting medications (metoprolol, acetaminophen, heparin, oxycodone, hydromorphone, ondansetron, ipratropium) create a high-risk scenario for pantoprazole administration. The primary hazards are drug-drug interactions, altered pharmacokinetics in renal impairment, and age-related vulnerability.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 162, "uid": "mimic-age-22059910-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of **carbamazepine** (500 mg BID) and the ordered **lorazepam** (1 mg IV). Carbamazepine is a potent inducer of CYP3A4 and CYP2C19, the primary enzymes responsible for lorazepam metabolism. This induction accelerates lorazepam clearance, reducing its plasma concentration and potentially compromising the intended sedative effect. Additionally, both drugs carry CNS-depressant properties, and their co-administration can produce additive respiratory depression, especially in a patient with acute respiratory failure and pulmonary embolism. The patient's age (67 years) further heightens vulnerability to such interactions.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 163, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27411876-Vancomycin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 21 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent lidocaine therapy. Vancomycin is renally eliminated, and its accumulation can precipitate acute kidney injury. Additionally, lidocaine is a known QT-prolonging agent, and vancomycin carries a dose-dependent risk of QT prolongation and torsades de pointes. The patient's other encephalopathy and ventilator-associated pneumonia further complicate safety.\\n\\n2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 164, "uid": "mimic-safe-23721604-Potassiumphosphate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the ordered Potassium phosphate (15 mmol IV) in a patient with multiple concurrent medications and underlying renal impairment. The patient's eGFR is 50 mL/min/1.73m2 (moderately reduced) and she is currently receiving Potassium chloride (20 mEq PO daily), Vancomycin (125 mg PO/NG QID), and Heparin (5000 units SC QID). These agents each carry a known risk of hyperkalemia, and the addition of Potassium phosphate intravenously - a direct potassium source - creates a synergistic, additive danger. The patient's age (82 years) further compromises renal potassium clearance, compounding the risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 165, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28661809-Ondansetron", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute and chronic respiratory failure with hypoxia, combined with concurrent use of fentanyl (opioid) and propofol (sedative), creates a high-risk scenario for respiratory depression. Ondansetron, a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist, can cause dose-dependent respiratory depression, particularly at higher doses or in the presence of other respiratory depressants. The patient's compromised respiratory status (hypoxia, pneumonia, COPD) further amplifies this hazard.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 166, "uid": "mimic-ddi-20611640-Methadone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of hydromorphone (a full mu-opioid agonist) and the newly ordered methadone (a full mu-opioid agonist with additional QT-prolonging properties) creates a direct drug-drug interaction hazard. Additionally, the patient's intracerebral hemorrhage with cerebral edema and hemiplegia, combined with the opioid's potential to depress the central nervous system and respiratory drive, elevates the risk of adverse outcomes. The presence of multiple other CNS-active agents (dexamethasone, levetiracetam, lidocaine, hydromorphone) further compounds the hazard.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 167, "uid": "mimic-safe-27525946-Hydroxyurea", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's active chronic duodenal ulcer with hemorrhage. Hydroxyurea, a myelosuppressive agent, can cause thrombocytopenia and bone marrow suppression, directly impairing hemostasis. In a patient with an ongoing gastrointestinal bleed, this added risk of platelet depletion and marrow suppression makes the drug unsafe. Additionally, the patient's thrombocytopenia (unspecified severity) further amplifies bleeding vulnerability, and the presence of sepsis and septic shock indicates a fragile physiological state that cannot tolerate additional hematologic stress.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 168, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28829452-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute respiratory failure with hypoxia, combined with a current regimen containing multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants (alprazolam, morphine, gabapentin, escitalopram, buspirone, and lorazepam itself), creates a high-risk scenario for additive respiratory depression. The addition of lorazepam to this polypharmacy list directly threatens airway patency and oxygenation.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 169, "uid": "mimic-ddi-25742920-Trazodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's hyperpotassemia (K+ 6.9 mEq/L) is the sole hazard. Trazodone, a serotonin antagonist/reuptake inhibitor, can cause dose-dependent QT prolongation and arrhythmias. In the setting of pre-existing hyperkalemia, the risk of life-threatening cardiac events (e.g., torsades de pointes) is markedly elevated. Additionally, the patient's chronic hepatitis C with cirrhosis (ALT/AST elevation, INR 1.5) indicates compromised hepatic function, which may further impair trazodone clearance and increase systemic exposure, compounding the arrhythmia risk. The concurrent use of tramadol and morphine (both serotonergic and opioid agents) also raises the potential for serotonin syndrome and additive CNS depression, though the primary concern remains the hyperkalemia-cardiotoxicity axis.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 170, "uid": "mimic-safe-22797747-Ipratropium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile contains multiple interacting hazards: advanced age (70 years), severe obesity (BMI 31.9), acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis, congestive heart failure, unspecified sleep apnea, and a complex polypharmacy regimen. The ordered ipratropium nebulizer solution (1 NEB, 4 times/day) is a bronchodilator that can exacerbate respiratory compromise, increase heart rate and myocardial oxygen demand, and may cause electrolyte disturbances (e.g., hyperkalemia) that are particularly dangerous in the setting of acute kidney injury and cardiac impairment. Additionally, the patient is on lidocaine, which is a class IB antiarrhythmic; ipratropium can blunt compensatory mechanisms, potentially unmasking lidocaine's proarrhythmic effects. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 171, "uid": "mimic-renal-21322534-Gabapentin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk", "Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 6 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (89 years). Gabapentin is renally eliminated; with such severe impairment, standard dosing leads to accumulation, increasing risks of sedation, respiratory depression, and falls. Additionally, the patient is on methadone (a CYP3A4 substrate) and metoprolol (a CYP2D6 substrate), and gabapentin may inhibit CYP3A4, creating a potential drug-drug interaction that could elevate methadone levels and worsen cardiac conduction. The combination of renal failure, age, and polypharmacy overrides any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 172, "uid": "mimic-age-22130791-Desmopressin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 15 mL/min/1.73m2) and melena create a direct hazard for desmopressin. Desmopressin is a synthetic V2-receptor agonist that promotes water retention and can precipitate hyponatremia, especially in the setting of renal impairment where dilutional hyponatremia is already a risk. Additionally, melena indicates gastrointestinal bleeding, and desmopressin's vasopressin-like action may exacerbate hypovolemia and worsen renal perfusion, risking further tubular necrosis. The patient's advanced age (88 years) and multiple comorbidities (NSTEMI, heart failure, respiratory failure, chronic kidney disease stage 4, encephalopathy, thrombocytopenia) compound the vulnerability, making the drug unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
