{"idx": 174, "uid": "mimic-safe-22081550-Ceftazidime", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - famotidine, citalopram, valproic acid, cefepime, metronidazole, and norepinephrine - create a high-risk drug-drug interaction with ceftazidime. Additionally, the patient's acute respiratory failure and anoxic brain damage indicate compromised respiratory reserve, and the presence of multiple interacting agents elevates the likelihood of adverse events.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 175, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27553957-Carvedilol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **moderately reduced renal function (eGFR 35 mL/min/1.73m2)** combined with **concurrent use of multiple interacting medications** (warfarin, labetalol, metoprolol, oxycodone, spironolactone, irbesartan, levothyroxine, insulin, glucagon, iron, aspirin, ezetimibe, bupropion, salbutamol). Adding carvedilol - a non-selective beta-blocker with alpha-1 blockade and mild diuretic properties - creates a high-risk scenario for additive bradycardia, hypotension, and electrolyte disturbances, especially in the setting of acute kidney failure and pre-existing heart failure. The patient's atrial fibrillation, congestive heart failure, and old myocardial infarction further amplify the danger of rate control and hemodynamic compromise.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 176, "uid": "mimic-safe-27488741-Clindamycin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute-on-chronic diastolic heart failure, chronic passive congestion of the liver, and extensive polypharmacy create a high-risk scenario for clindamycin. The primary hazards are hepatotoxicity from clindamycin in the setting of hepatic congestion, additive respiratory depression with morphine and benzodiazepines, and potential for QT prolongation or arrhythmia given the cardiac impairment and concurrent medications.\\n\\n2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 177, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22196214-Tizanidine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes multiple CNS depressants (diazepam, morphine, hydromorphone, gabapentin, baclofen, acetaminophen) and a potent anticoagulant (heparin). Tizanidine, a central alpha-2 agonist, produces sedation and can further depress the central nervous system. The combined pharmacodynamic effect of additive CNS depression and the anticoagulant's bleeding risk create a hazard that overrides any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 178, "uid": "mimic-age-28861356-Ketorolac", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's advanced age (80 years), mild renal impairment (eGFR 61 mL/min/1.73m2), and concurrent use of metoprolol, acetaminophen, and hydromorphone. Ketorolac, a non-selective NSAID, is contraindicated in this profile due to additive nephrotoxicity, increased bleeding risk from antiplatelet effects, and potential for additive CNS depression with opioids and beta-blockers.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 179, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22251969-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 12 mL/min/1.73m2). Oxycodone is primarily renally eliminated as its active metabolite (noroxycodone). In severe renal impairment, clearance is markedly reduced, leading to drug accumulation and elevated risk of CNS depression, respiratory depression, and sedation. Additionally, the patient is concurrently on multiple CNS-active medications (lorazepam, warfarin, bupropion, miconazole, methylprednisolone, carvedilol, senna, insulin, salbutamol, fluticasone) that can synergistically depress the central nervous system. The combination of renal impairment and polypharmacy creates a high-risk scenario for opioid toxicity.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 180, "uid": "mimic-safe-21636229-Lidocaine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's current use of **heparin** (5000 units SC twice daily) and **oxycodone** (10 mg PO/NG). Lidocaine, a class IB antiarrhythmic, is known to prolong the QT interval and can potentiate the proarrhythmic effects of other drugs. Heparin, while not directly interacting with lidocaine, is associated with an increased risk of bleeding, and lidocaine's potential to cause hypotension or arrhythmia could exacerbate this risk. Oxycodone, a mu-opioid agonist, can independently prolong the QT interval; co-administration with lidocaine may produce additive QT prolongation, raising the risk of torsades de pointes. Additionally, lidocaine is metabolized primarily by CYP3A4, and oxycodone is also a CYP3A4 substrate, though the interaction is not well-documented; the combined use of two QT-prolonging agents in a patient with portal hypertension and ascites (compromised hemodynamics) creates a clinically significant proarrhythmic hazard.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 181, "uid": "mimic-safe-24490144-Cefazolin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's concurrent use of **nitroglycerin** (a coronary vasodilator) and **phenylephrine** (an alpha-1 agonist vasoconstrictor) with the ordered **cefazolin**. This combination creates a high-risk drug-drug interaction scenario.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 182, "uid": "mimic-age-23403708-Diphenhydramine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's advanced age (82 years) and the concurrent use of multiple central nervous system (CNS) and cardiovascular agents. Diphenhydramine, a potent antihistamine with strong anticholinergic properties, can cause sedation, confusion, and tachycardia. When co-administered with hydromorphone (opioid), naloxone (opioid antagonist), phenylephrine (vasopressor), metoclopramide (prokinetic with anticholinergic activity), and salbutamol (beta-agonist), the cumulative CNS and cardiovascular effects are amplified. This polypharmacy, combined with age-related pharmacodynamic sensitivity, creates a high risk of excessive sedation, respiratory depression, and cardiovascular instability.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 183, "uid": "mimic-safe-20093566-Fluticasonetopical", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 5 mL/min/1.73m2) and alcoholic hepatic failure without coma are the primary hazards. Fluticasone, a topical corticosteroid, is not systemically absorbed, but in the setting of severe renal impairment, any residual absorption could lead to accumulation of its active metabolites, increasing the risk of hyperglycemia, immunosuppression, and electrolyte disturbances. Additionally, the patient's hepatic impairment may alter the metabolism of other co-administered drugs (e.g., octreotide, pantoprazole) and could theoretically affect the local pharmacokinetics of the topical formulation. The combination of these two organ failures creates a net risk that overrides the general safety of topical fluticasone.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 184, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27962747-Gabapentin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of hydromorphone (20 mg IVPCA) and the proposed gabapentin. Both agents are central nervous system depressants, and their co-administration produces additive sedation, respiratory depression, and potential for excessive sedation. The patient's underlying pancreatitis with pseudocyst may further compromise respiratory reserve, amplifying the risk. Additionally, the patient is on hydralazine, which can cause orthostatic hypotension; gabapentin may potentiate this effect, though the primary concern remains the drug-drug interaction with hydromorphone.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 185, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27494880-Clopidogrel", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple interacting medications - including fluoxetine, valproic acid, hydrocodone, gabapentin, and ziprasidone - with the newly ordered clopidogrel creates a high-risk polypharmacy scenario. Additionally, the patient's chronic lymphocytic leukemia (B-cell type) without remission introduces a bleeding-prone state, and the presence of a coronary angioplasty graft amplifies the need for antithrombotic therapy. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
