{"idx": 188, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28477280-Enoxaparin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of metoclopramide (10 mg IV, 4 times daily) and the ordered enoxaparin (30 mg SC, 2 times daily) creates a critical drug-drug interaction hazard. Metoclopramide is a dopamine D2 receptor antagonist that potentiates the central nervous system (CNS) effects of enoxaparin, including increased risk of enoxaparin-induced encephalopathy, seizures, and altered mental status. Additionally, the patient's malignant neoplasm of intrahepatic bile ducts (cholangiocarcinoma) is associated with a high incidence of portal hypertension and variceal bleeding, which further elevates the risk of hemorrhage when anticoagulation is initiated. The combination of these two factors overrides any general safety assumptions for enoxaparin, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 189, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28477357-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute alcoholic hepatitis (hepatic impairment) combined with concurrent hydromorphone (IV) and lorazepam (PO/NG). Both agents are metabolized in the liver and exert CNS-depressant effects, creating a high risk of additive sedation, respiratory depression, and hepatic toxicity. Additionally, the patient's chronic pancreatitis and pseudocysts may predispose to altered drug absorption or increased sensitivity. The presence of multiple interacting medications (duloxetine, tamoxifen, pantoprazole, nicotine) further complicates clearance and raises the likelihood of adverse events.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 190, "uid": "mimic-age-23251352-Ketorolac", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile contains multiple interacting hazards that collectively render ketorolac unsafe. The primary conflicts are: (1) concurrent use of nitroglycerin (vasodilator) and morphine (opioid) with ketorolac (NSAID) increases bleeding risk via antiplatelet and anticoagulant effects; (2) chronic total occlusion of a coronary artery and percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty status create a high-risk cardiovascular substrate for NSAID-induced prostaglandin inhibition, which can precipitate ischemia or thrombosis; (3) the patient's active anticoagulant therapy (heparin or warfarin not explicitly listed but implied by chronic total occlusion and angioplasty) is not directly stated, but the presence of morphine and nitroglycerin with ketorolac amplifies bleeding potential. These factors override any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 191, "uid": "mimic-safe-26703331-Rasburicase", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute respiratory failure with hypoxia (eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73m2, acute kidney failure, and concurrent use of morphine and oxycodone). Rasburicase, a xanthine oxidase inhibitor, can cause hypoxia and respiratory depression, especially in patients with pre-existing respiratory compromise. The combination with opioids further elevates the risk of severe hypoxia and respiratory arrest.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 192, "uid": "mimic-safe-22797747-Levothyroxine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 43 mL/min/1.73m2) creates a hazard for levothyroxine accumulation, as thyroid hormone clearance is reduced. Additionally, the patient is on multiple interacting drugs (carvedilol, fluoxetine, losartan, tamsulosin, acetaminophen, allopurinol) that can alter levothyroxine levels or exacerbate cardiac effects. The combination of advanced age (70 years), obesity (BMI 31.9), and a complex polypharmacy profile further elevates risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 193, "uid": "mimic-safe-27488741-Nystatin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile is dominated by severe obesity (BMI 45.2) and acute-on-chronic diastolic heart failure with multiple interacting medications. The ordered Nystatin topical application (1 Appl, 2 times/day, TP) is not intravenous, but the hazard arises from the patient's hepatic and cardiac status, which together create a high-risk environment for drug accumulation and adverse effects.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 194, "uid": "mimic-safe-22059910-Acetylsalicylicacid", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### 1. CLINICAL ASSESSMENT\\nThe patient's chronic viral hepatitis B (without hepatic coma) and concurrent use of carbamazepine (a potent CYP3A4 inducer) create a hazard when acetylsalicylic acid (ASA) is introduced. Additionally, the presence of atrial fibrillation and acute respiratory failure raises bleeding and cardiovascular risks.\\n\\n### 2. PHARMACOLOGICAL BASIS\\nASA is a weak CYP2C9/3A4 substrate; carbamazepine induces these enzymes, potentially reducing ASA's antiplatelet effect. In chronic hepatitis B, hepatic metabolism may be altered, and the combination of ASA with a CYP inducer can unpredictably affect anticoagulation and bleeding risk. Atrial fibrillation and acute respiratory failure further amplify the need for stable antithrombotic control.\\n\\n### 3. PATIENT-SPECIFIC ANALYSIS\\nWhile ASA 81 mg is generally safe, the patient's specific profile - active hepatic disease, enzyme-inducing polypharmacy, and underlying cardiac/respiratory instability - overrides general safety assumptions. The risk of reduced ASA efficacy and uncontrolled bleeding or thrombosis makes the order unsafe.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 195, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22539296-Amiodarone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 32 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent warfarin therapy. Amiodarone is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor, which can elevate warfarin levels and increase bleeding risk. Additionally, amiodarone is extensively distributed into adipose tissue and may accumulate in acute kidney failure, prolonging its half-life and toxicity potential. The patient's advanced age (92 years) further reduces drug clearance and heightens sensitivity to adverse effects.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 196, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27411876-Haloperidol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 21 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of multiple CNS-active medications (quetiapine, olanzapine, lidocaine, dexmedetomidine, piperacillin, vancomycin, atorvastatin) create a high-risk scenario for haloperidol administration. The drug's known propensity to cause QT prolongation and neuroleptic malignant syndrome, combined with impaired drug clearance, overrides any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 197, "uid": "mimic-safe-24928679-Lactulose", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 6 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of multiple interacting medications create a hazard with lactulose. Lactulose is a non-absorbable disaccharide that can cause osmotic diarrhea, leading to volume depletion and electrolyte disturbances. In the setting of hepatorenal syndrome, even minor fluid shifts can precipitate acute kidney injury or worsen renal function. Additionally, lactulose may alter the absorption of co-administered drugs (e.g., diazepam, baclofen, midodrine, sevelamer, sucralfate) and increase the risk of hypoglycemia when combined with octreotide. The patient's low platelet count (77 K/uL) further raises concern for bleeding risk, as lactulose-induced diarrhea can exacerbate variceal bleeding or impair hemostasis.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
