{"idx": 207, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24656677-Dextromethorphan", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (81 years) combined with moderate renal impairment (eGFR 50 mL/min/1.73m2) and a complex polypharmacy regimen. Dextromethorphan is a serotonergic agent that inhibits CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, and its active metabolite MPP+ is renally excreted. In this vulnerable patient, the risk of serotonin syndrome (triggered by concurrent serotonergic drugs such as buspirone, paroxetine, ciprofloxacin) and additive CNS depression (with propranolol, ranitidine, omeprazole, and others) is markedly elevated. Additionally, the low body weight (59 kg) and acute kidney failure further reduce clearance, increasing the likelihood of toxicity.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 208, "uid": "mimic-safe-22205327-Acetylcysteine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 15 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of multiple nephrotoxic agents (e.g., fentanyl, epinephrine, phenylephrine, heparin, calcium gluconate, potassium chloride) create a high-risk environment for further renal injury. Acetylcysteine, while generally safe, carries a known risk of acute kidney injury, especially in patients with pre-existing renal impairment. The combination of severe renal impairment, polypharmacy with nephrotoxic drugs, and the need for intravenous administration of acetylcysteine (which can cause local irritation and systemic adverse effects) overrides any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 209, "uid": "mimic-safe-22797747-Vancomycin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (70 years) combined with a high body weight (101 kg, BMI 31.9) and the concurrent use of multiple interacting medications. Vancomycin, a glycopeptide antibiotic, is known to cause dose-dependent nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity, and its clearance is reduced in elderly patients. The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis further compromises renal reserve, making accumulation of vancomycin metabolites and its toxic metabolites (e.g., vancomycin N-acyl side chain) more likely. Additionally, the patient is on fluoxetine (a strong CYP2C9 inhibitor) and warfarin (not listed but implied by heparin use), which can alter vancomycin pharmacokinetics and increase bleeding risk. The prescribed dose (125 mg four times daily) is standard for normal renal function, but in this setting the risk of toxicity and adverse drug reactions outweighs any benefit, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 210, "uid": "mimic-age-27505812-Ketorolac", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - nitroglycerin, phenylephrine, morphine, oxycodone, metoclopramide, cefazolin, albumin, and others - create a high-risk pharmacodynamic profile when combined with ketorolac. The primary hazards are additive hypotension (from nitroglycerin, phenylephrine, morphine, oxycodone, and ketorolac) and potential for occult bleeding (from ketorolac's antiplatelet effect in the setting of coronary atherosclerosis and concurrent anticoagulant/antiplatelet use). Additionally, the patient's intermediate coronary syndrome and coronary atherosclerosis increase the risk of ischemic events with NSAID use.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 211, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24470193-Sotalol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - oxycodone, morphine, hydralazine, lisinopril, phenytoin, and senna - create a high-risk pharmacodynamic and pharmacokinetic conflict with sotalol. Additionally, the presence of atrial fibrillation and intracerebral hemorrhage amplifies the hazard.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 212, "uid": "mimic-safe-23983182-Piperacillin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent administration of piperacillin with alteplase (1 mg IV daily) in a patient with chronic heart failure and coronary atherosclerosis. Piperacillin is a broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibiotic that can inhibit the metabolism of alteplase, a thrombolytic agent, thereby increasing the risk of excessive bleeding. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (80 years) and mild renal impairment (eGFR 61) further amplify the bleeding risk, as both factors are known to impair hemostatic reserve and drug clearance.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 213, "uid": "mimic-safe-25172300-Iron", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 29 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent use of oxycodone (opioid analgesic) and irbesartan (ARB). Adding oral iron (325 mg twice daily) introduces a drug-drug interaction that can exacerbate renal impairment and increase the risk of opioid toxicity.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 214, "uid": "mimic-safe-27617929-Midazolam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 40 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with the prescribed midazolam dose of 100 mg IV. Midazolam is hepatically metabolized, but in severe renal impairment, drug accumulation and prolonged sedation can occur. The dose of 100 mg is extremely high for a 60-year-old with multiple organ failures, and the absence of a dose adjustment (e.g., 50 mg) makes this regimen unsafe.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 215, "uid": "mimic-safe-20192635-Glucagon", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **moderately reduced renal function (eGFR 35 mL/min/1.73m2)** combined with **advanced age (80 years)** and **multiple interacting medications**. The ordered glucagon (1 mg IM) is intended to counteract insulin overdose, but its safety is compromised by the following interplay:\\n\\n1. **Renal Impairment**: Glucagon clearance is partially renal-dependent; in moderate impairment, glucagon half-life may be prolonged, increasing the risk of accumulation and hypoglycemia rebound. Additionally, glucagon can induce counter-regulatory stress responses that may worsen renal perfusion in acute kidney failure.\\n2. **Age**: Elderly patients have blunted counter-regulatory responses, higher baseline insulin sensitivity, and increased susceptibility to hypoglycemia. The combination of age and renal impairment amplifies the risk of severe hypoglycemia, which can precipitate seizures, coma, or cardiac arrhythmias.\\n3. **Polypharmacy**: The patient is on numerous drugs (insulin, antihypertensives, anticoagulants, anxiolytics, statins, etc.). Glucagon can potentiate hypoglycemia with insulin, and its use may alter the pharmacokinetics of other agents (e.g., amlodipine, losartan, paroxetine) via unknown mechanisms, creating unpredictable interactions.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 216, "uid": "mimic-safe-25135483-Lidocaine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's current medication list, which includes multiple drugs that can independently depress the central nervous system (CNS) and, when combined with lidocaine, produce additive or synergistic CNS depression. The specific drugs are morphine, gabapentin, hydromorphone, acetaminophen, duloxetine, and piperacillin. These agents, when co-administered with lidocaine, increase the risk of excessive sedation, respiratory depression, and altered mental status. Additionally, the patient's acute pancreatitis may alter lidocaine metabolism or clearance, though the primary driver is the polypharmacy interaction.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 217, "uid": "mimic-safe-28861356-Cyanocobalamin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's **ileostomy** (large/small bowel absence) combined with **advanced age (80 years)**. Cyanocobalamin (vitamin B12) administered intramuscularly is intended for systemic absorption, but in the setting of an ileostomy, the terminal ileum - the primary site of intrinsic factor-mediated B12 absorption - is bypassed. This creates a **pharmacodynamic conflict**: the drug is given intramuscularly, but the anatomical defect renders the route ineffective, and the patient's age further reduces mucosal integrity and absorption capacity, increasing the risk of B12 deficiency and subsequent neurological complications.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true,\n  \"Chronic Condition Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 218, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27996267-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of hydromorphone (0.25 mg IV) and the newly ordered oxycodone (5-10 mg PO/NG) creates a high-risk opioid-opioid interaction. Additionally, the patient's moderate renal impairment (eGFR 30 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (69 years) further amplify the hazard.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 219, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22675517-Acetylsalicylicacid", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of multiple medications with known or potential interactions with acetylsalicylic acid (ASA). Specifically, the presence of oxycodone, morphine, metoclopramide, meperidine, ranitidine, and ketorolac creates a complex pharmacodynamic and pharmacokinetic milieu that elevates bleeding risk and may alter ASA metabolism.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 220, "uid": "mimic-safe-25135483-Nicotine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes morphine, gabapentin, hydromorphone, duloxetine, tamoxifen, and gemfibrozil - all potent CNS depressants and/or CYP3A4/2D6 substrates. Nicotine, a nicotinic agonist, can enhance CNS depression via additive sedation and respiratory depression, and it may also inhibit CYP2D6, raising tamoxifen's active metabolite levels. The combination of multiple opioids, gabapentin, and serotonergic agents with nicotine creates a high-risk profile for respiratory compromise, excessive sedation, and altered drug metabolism, overriding any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 221, "uid": "mimic-ddi-20214994-Quetiapine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 59 mL/min/1.73m2) and multiple concurrent medications (magnesium sulfate, calcium gluconate, lidocaine, furosemide, amiodarone, diltiazem, metoprolol, hydromorphone, potassium chloride) create a high-risk scenario for quetiapine administration. The drug's known QT-prolonging effect, combined with electrolyte disturbances (hypomagnesemia, hypokalemia, hyperkalemia from potassium chloride) and drug-drug interactions (amiodarone, diltiazem, lidocaine, metoprolol, hydromorphone) in a patient with compromised renal function, overrides any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 222, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27660781-Fentanyl", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's hepatic impairment (abscess of liver) with INR 2.0 and ALT/AST mildly elevated creates a direct hazard for fentanyl, a potent opioid that can cause respiratory depression and requires hepatic metabolism. Additionally, the concurrent use of hydromorphone (1-2 mg IV) and fentanyl (25-100 mcg IV) introduces additive CNS and respiratory depression risk. The patient's paralytic ileus and adult failure to thrive further compromise respiratory reserve, compounding the danger.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
