{"idx": 224, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22741225-Ziprasidone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's current medication list, which includes Fluoxetine (10 mg daily), Risperidone (0.5 mg daily), and Valproic acid (500 mg daily). These three agents each carry a known risk of QT prolongation, and their concurrent use creates a synergistic proarrhythmic effect. The addition of ziprasidone, another potent QT-prolonging antipsychotic, compounds this danger. Additionally, the patient's mild renal impairment (eGFR 69 mL/min) and obesity (BMI 37.1) further elevate the risk of drug accumulation and adverse cardiac events.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 225, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29176490-Trazodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of citalopram (20 mg daily) and trazodone (25 mg) creates a pharmacodynamic interaction that elevates the risk of QT-interval prolongation and torsades de pointes. Additionally, the patient's coronary atherosclerosis with angina pectoris and current use of clopidogrel and aspirin further heighten the risk of bleeding, as trazodone can impair platelet aggregation and additively increase anticoagulant effect.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 226, "uid": "mimic-ddi-26173805-Acetylsalicylicacid", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the concurrent use of acetylsalicylic acid (ASA) with rivaroxaban, a direct oral anticoagulant, and hydrocodone, an opioid analgesic. The patient's chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) and type 2 diabetes mellitus with diabetic polyneuropathy further complicate the risk profile.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 227, "uid": "mimic-safe-25135483-Piperacillin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and anticoagulant medications, which collectively amplify the risk of central nervous system depression and bleeding when combined with piperacillin. The specific drug-drug interactions are as follows:\\n\\n1. **Morphine + Hydromorphone + Piperacillin**: Piperacillin can potentiate opioid-induced respiratory depression and sedation, especially when combined with morphine and hydromorphone. This is a well-documented pharmacodynamic interaction.\\n2. **Gabapentin + Piperacillin**: Both agents depress the CNS, leading to additive sedation, dizziness, and impaired psychomotor function.\\n3. **Heparin + Piperacillin**: Piperacillin may inhibit the inactivation of heparin by antithrombin III, increasing anticoagulant effect and bleeding risk.\\n4. **Gemfibrozil + Piperacillin**: Gemfibrozil can reduce piperacillin clearance via CYP3A4 inhibition, raising piperacillin plasma concentrations and toxicity risk.\\n5. **Duloxetine + Piperacillin**: Both drugs affect serotonin and GABA pathways; combined use may exacerbate CNS depression.\\n6. **Tamoxifen + Piperacillin**: Piperacillin can inhibit CYP2C9, potentially elevating tamoxifen metabolite levels and increasing adverse effects.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
