{"idx": 229, "uid": "mimic-safe-24982426-Acetaminophen", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile contains multiple interacting hazards that collectively render the acetaminophen order unsafe. The primary conflicts are (1) moderate renal impairment (eGFR 46 mL/min) combined with concurrent use of multiple nephrotoxic agents (e.g., vancomycin, metronidazole, gabapentin, heparin, phenylephrine, hydromorphone, atropine, trazodone) and (2) advanced age (81 years) with polypharmacy, which amplifies the risk of drug accumulation and toxicity. Additionally, the IV route for acetaminophen in a patient with compromised renal function and electrolyte disturbances (e.g., potassium chloride, magnesium sulfate) raises concerns for local tissue injury and systemic adverse effects.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 230, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29600294-Amiodarone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple interacting drugs (fluoxetine, ziprasidone, oxycodone, valproic acid, prednisone, levothyroxine, lidocaine, guaifenesin, insulin, pantoprazole, glucagon) combined with moderate renal impairment (eGFR 37) and a high BMI (35.9) creates a high-risk scenario for amiodarone administration. The primary hazard is additive QT prolongation and bradycardia from amiodarone with fluoxetine, ziprasidone, and lidocaine; additionally, amiodarone's hepatotoxicity and thyroid effects are amplified by valproic acid and levothyroxine. The IV bolus route further elevates the risk of arrhythmia and hypotension in a patient with congestive heart failure and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 231, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28676446-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent use of oxycodone with hydromorphone (both mu-opioid agonists) and the patient's advanced age (82 years). This combination creates a high risk of additive respiratory depression, sedation, and falls. Additionally, the patient's recurrent pneumonia history and frailty from a femoral neck fracture further amplify vulnerability to respiratory compromise and infection.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 232, "uid": "mimic-safe-28258130-Nicotine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 32 mL/min/1.73m2) and multiple concurrent medications that directly affect electrolyte balance and cardiovascular stability create a hazard with nicotine administration. Nicotine is metabolized by CYP1A2, but the primary concern is its pharmacodynamic effects on the cardiovascular and renal systems in the setting of pre-existing renal impairment and polypharmacy.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 233, "uid": "mimic-safe-26703331-Ipratropium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute respiratory failure with hypoxia, moderate renal impairment (eGFR 48), and concurrent use of oxycodone and morphine create a hazard when adding ipratropium. The primary conflict is between the patient's compromised respiratory status and the potential for additive respiratory depression from the combination of an opioid agonist and an anticholinergic bronchodilator.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 234, "uid": "mimic-ddi-25177949-Tizanidine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's extensive polypharmacy list - including hydromorphone, lisinopril, furosemide, acetaminophen, and multiple laxatives - creates a high-risk drug-drug interaction profile. Tizanidine, a central alpha-2 agonist, is contraindicated with drugs that depress the central nervous system or cause additive hypotension. The concurrent use of hydromorphone (opioid) and tizanidine (sedative) produces additive CNS depression, while lisinopril and furosemide (ACEi + loop diuretic) synergistically lower blood pressure, increasing the risk of severe hypotension. Additionally, the patient's multiple laxatives (polyethylene glycol, bisacodyl, lactulose, senna, fleet enema) may alter tizanidine absorption or cause electrolyte disturbances that further compromise safety. These factors override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 235, "uid": "mimic-safe-27553957-Ezetimibe", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **moderately reduced renal function (eGFR 35 mL/min/1.73m2)** combined with **concurrent warfarin therapy**. Ezetimibe is renally eliminated as a prodrug (ezetimibe is the active moiety; its precursor is renally cleared). In renal impairment, the precursor may accumulate, potentially increasing the risk of ezetimibe exposure and, although not a direct interaction, the presence of warfarin creates a scenario where any subtle change in drug levels could alter anticoagulation. Additionally, the patient's atrial fibrillation and multiple cardiac comorbidities (CHF, old MI, coronary atherosclerosis) mean that any proarrhythmic or hemodynamic effect of ezetimibe (though rare) could be clinically significant. The combination of renal impairment and warfarin also raises the possibility of additive bleeding risk, even if ezetimibe itself does not potentiate warfarin.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 236, "uid": "mimic-age-20973395-Chlorpheniramine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants - including oxycodone, hydromorphone, and piperacillin - combined with the proposed chlorpheniramine creates a hazard of additive sedation and respiratory depression. Additionally, the patient's active opioid use (oxycodone, hydromorphone) and the presence of generalized peritonitis with hypotension raise concerns about altered pharmacokinetics and increased opioid sensitivity. The antihistamine's anticholinergic properties may also interact with piperacillin, potentially affecting gastrointestinal motility and drug absorption.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 237, "uid": "mimic-age-22130791-Scopolamine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile is a convergence of advanced age (88 years), severe renal impairment (eGFR 13 mL/min/1.73m2), multiple concurrent CNS depressants (fentanyl, propofol, hydromorphone, haloperidol, ondansetron, glycopyrronium, hyoscyamine), and a current platelet count of 81 \u00d7 10^9/L. The ordered scopolamine, a potent anticholinergic agent, creates a triple hazard: additive CNS depression, risk of acute kidney injury from anticholinergic-induced myopathy and hypotension, and exacerbation of melena via anticholinergic-induced constipation and gastrointestinal hypomotility. The patient's fragile hemodynamic state (hypovolemic shock, acute respiratory failure, hypoxia) further amplifies the danger.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 238, "uid": "mimic-ddi-20291550-Naproxen", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of multiple drugs that each independently increase the risk of gastrointestinal bleeding, combined with the presence of an open femur fracture (which predisposes to occult bleeding). Naproxen, a non-selective NSAID, further amplifies this risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 239, "uid": "mimic-safe-20364112-Potassiumphosphate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's chronic duodenal ulcer with perforation, which creates a high-risk gastrointestinal environment for potassium phosphate IV administration. The patient is already receiving multiple intravenous electrolyte and drug products (e.g., potassium chloride, calcium gluconate, magnesium sulfate, lidocaine, heparin, piperacillin, vancomycin, fluconazole, esomeprazole). Adding potassium phosphate to this regimen elevates the risk of local tissue necrosis, perforation, and systemic electrolyte disturbances (hyperkalemia, hypercalcemia, hypermagnesemia). The perforated ulcer also predisposes to sepsis and peritonitis, which are exacerbated by IV potassium phosphate's potential to cause mucosal injury and chemical burns.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 240, "uid": "mimic-safe-28166872-Cefazolin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute respiratory failure, methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) pneumonia, and concurrent use of multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants (fentanyl, midazolam, propofol, morphine, levetiracetam, ondansetron). Adding cefazolin introduces a new drug that can further depress respiratory drive, especially in a patient already on high-dose opioid and sedative combinations.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 241, "uid": "mimic-safe-21791856-Ipratropium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 14 mL/min/1.73m2, acute kidney failure) and concurrent use of multiple interacting medications create a high-risk scenario for unsafe administration of ipratropium. The primary hazards are drug-drug interactions and the need for dose adjustment in renal failure.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 242, "uid": "mimic-age-21477991-Diazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and anticoagulant medications creates a high-risk pharmacodynamic interaction with diazepam. Specifically, the combination of diazepam with propranolol (beta-blocker), valproic acid (anticonvulsant), oxycodone, morphine, and ondansetron (serotonin antagonist) produces additive central nervous system depression and respiratory depression. Additionally, the patient is on heparin, and diazepam can potentiate the anticoagulant effect, increasing bleeding risk. These overlapping hazards override any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 243, "uid": "mimic-safe-20214994-Cefazolin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's current medication list, particularly the intravenous magnesium sulfate (2 g) and potassium chloride (40 mEq). Cefazolin, a cephalosporin antibiotic, carries a known risk of hypotensive and proarrhythmic effects, especially when co-administered with agents that can cause or exacerbate electrolyte disturbances. The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis further compromises renal clearance of drugs and electrolytes, amplifying the risk of additive toxicity.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 244, "uid": "mimic-safe-27488741-Magnesiumsulfate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile contains multiple interacting hazards that collectively render the magnesium sulfate order unsafe. The primary conflicts are: (1) **severe obesity (BMI 45.2)**, which increases the volume of distribution and risk of hypotension; (2) **acute-on-chronic diastolic heart failure** with congestive heart failure, predisposing to hypotension and cardiac arrhythmia; (3) **concurrent use of furosemide (250 mg IV)**, which promotes magnesium loss via renal excretion; (4) **hepatic congestion (INR 2.1, total bilirubin 1.6 mg/dL)**, impairing magnesium clearance and raising the risk of toxicity; (5) **multiple CNS depressants** (diazepam, morphine, quetiapine, hydroxyzine, clindamycin, metoprolol) that can potentiate magnesium's sedative and respiratory-depressant effects; and (6) **renal impairment (eGFR 61 mL/min/1.73m2)**, which reduces magnesium excretion and increases serum concentration risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 245, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22051341-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of multiple CNS-active medications - gabapentin, pregabalin, duloxetine, hydromorphone, and lorazepam - which collectively produce additive central nervous system (CNS) depression. This polypharmacy, combined with the patient's dehydration and colostomy status, creates a high-risk scenario for respiratory depression, sedation, and altered mental status.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 246, "uid": "mimic-safe-27738145-Phenylephrine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's current medication list, specifically the presence of fentanyl (opioid) and propofol (benzodiazepine). Phenylephrine, a direct-acting alpha-1 adrenergic agonist, is intended to raise blood pressure. However, in a patient already under opioid and benzodiazepine sedation, the pressor effect can paradoxically cause tachycardia, reflex bradycardia, and hemodynamic instability. Additionally, the patient's acute respiratory failure and ventilator-associated pneumonia create a fragile respiratory state; any autonomic perturbation may compromise ventilation and oxygenation. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions about phenylephrine monotherapy.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 247, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22987108-Amiodarone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severe hepatic impairment (cirrhosis, INR 1.9, total bilirubin 26.9 mg/dL) and severely reduced renal function (eGFR 19 mL/min/1.73m2) create a dual hazard. Amiodarone is predominantly hepatically metabolized (CYP3A4, CYP2C9) and has a long half-life; accumulation in cirrhosis can precipitate hepatotoxicity, while renal impairment may delay clearance of its metabolites. Additionally, the patient is on multiple interacting drugs (e.g., midodrine, metoprolol, rifaximin, nystatin, vancomycin, meropenem, heparin, potassium chloride, calcium gluconate, insulin, ondansetron, pantoprazole, lactulose, senna, bisacodyl, docusate, methimazole). Amiodarone is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor, which can elevate concentrations of co-administered drugs, increasing toxicity risk. The IV bolus route further raises the likelihood of arrhythmogenic or hypotensive adverse effects in a patient with hypovolemic shock, atrial flutter, and atherosclerotic heart disease. These overlapping vulnerabilities override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 248, "uid": "mimic-safe-28166872-Ipratropium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - fentanyl, midazolam, propofol, lorazepam, morphine, hydralazine, and phenylephrine - all possess central nervous system (CNS) depressant properties. Adding ipratropium, an anticholinergic agent, creates a pharmacodynamic interaction that can potentiate sedation, respiratory depression, and hypotension. Additionally, the patient's acute respiratory failure and methicillin-resistant pneumonia (S. aureus) indicate a fragile respiratory status; any further compromise from drug-induced depression is unacceptable.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 249, "uid": "mimic-safe-20199380-Polyethyleneglycol3350", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes multiple central nervous system depressants (clonazepam, oxycodone, hydromorphone) and a serotonergic agent (duloxetine). Polyethylene glycol (PEG) is a bulk-forming laxative that can cause electrolyte disturbances (e.g., hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia) and, when combined with CNS depressants, may potentiate sedation and dizziness. The concurrent use of PEG with these drugs elevates the risk of additive CNS depression and potential cardiac arrhythmia due to electrolyte shifts, especially in a patient with a history of accidental organ laceration (surgical risk factor). The dosage (17 g) is standard, but the hazard arises from the drug-drug interaction with the existing regimen.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 250, "uid": "mimic-safe-23721604-Acetaminophen", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's advanced age (82 years), moderate renal impairment (eGFR 56 mL/min/1.73m2), and polypharmacy (13 concurrent medications) create a high-risk scenario for acetaminophen toxicity. The recommended dose of 650 mg PO/NG for two days exceeds the safe ceiling for this vulnerable profile, and the presence of multiple interacting drugs further elevates hepatotoxicity risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 251, "uid": "mimic-safe-24982426-Amoxicillin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of lidocaine, a class IB antiarrhythmic, and amoxicillin creates a hazard. Amoxicillin can inhibit CYP3A4, the primary enzyme responsible for lidocaine metabolism, potentially elevating lidocaine plasma concentrations and increasing the risk of lidocaine toxicity (e.g., arrhythmias, seizures). Additionally, the patient's moderate renal impairment (eGFR 50 mL/min) and advanced age (81 years) further heighten susceptibility to drug accumulation and adverse effects. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, rendering the amoxicillin order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 252, "uid": "mimic-age-24885579-Paroxetine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (85 years) combined with moderate renal impairment (eGFR 44 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of multiple interacting medications. Paroxetine, a potent CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 inhibitor, will elevate the systemic exposure of several co-administered drugs, including ciprofloxacin (CYP1A2/3C4), metronidazole (CYP1A2), omeprazole (CYP2C19), and mirtazapine (CYP2D6). Additionally, paroxetine's serotonergic activity synergizes with the patient's current use of mirtazapine and ciprofloxacin, raising the risk of serotonin syndrome. The patient's renal impairment further compromises drug clearance, and her age amplifies vulnerability to CNS depression and falls. These overlapping factors override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 253, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24698912-Alteplase", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 10 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of multiple nephrotoxic agents (e.g., amlodipine, vancomycin, neomycin, polyethylene glycol, senna, bisacodyl) create a high-risk scenario for alteplase administration. Alteplase is a thrombolytic that requires intact renal function to clear metabolites and avoid systemic bleeding; in severe renal impairment, half-life prolongation and accumulation increase hemorrhagic risk. Additionally, the patient's sepsis, acidosis, and hypo-osmolality further destabilize hemostasis, compounding the danger. The combination of renal failure and polypharmacy overrides any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 254, "uid": "mimic-safe-25239799-Acetaminophen", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### 1. CLINICAL ASSESSMENT\\nThe patient's profile reveals multiple interacting hazards: advanced age (78 years), aortic valve disorders, Mobitz II atrioventricular block, thrombocytopenia, and concurrent use of acetylsalicylic acid (ASA), citalopram, metoprolol, sulfasalazine, and simvastatin. The addition of acetaminophen creates a cumulative risk of hepatotoxicity, bleeding, and cardiac instability.\\n\\n### 2. PHARMACOLOGICAL BASIS\\nAcetaminophen is metabolized to NAPQI, which depletes glutathione and can cause liver injury. In the presence of sulfasalazine (a sulfonamide that inhibits N-acetyltransferase), the formation of NAPQI increases, raising hepatotoxicity risk. Additionally, acetaminophen may potentiate the anticoagulant effect of ASA and citalopram, elevating bleeding risk. In patients with pre-existing thrombocytopenia, even minor bleeding diathesis is amplified.\\n\\n### 3. PATIENT-SPECIFIC ANALYSIS\\nGeneral safety assumptions for acetaminophen (e.g., standard doses, no hepatic impairment) are overridden by this patient's specific profile: age-related reduced drug clearance, concurrent hepatotoxic agents, and bleeding diathesis. The combination of these factors makes even a short course of acetaminophen unsafe, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 255, "uid": "mimic-ddi-20437651-Carbamazepine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's concurrent use of lamotrigine (150 mg daily) and the newly ordered carbamazepine (300 mg daily). Both are CYP3A4 substrates and CYP3A4 inhibitors, creating a drug-drug interaction that elevates lamotrigine exposure and increases seizure-threshold lowering risk. Additionally, the patient's atrial fibrillation and multiple respiratory impairments (pneumonia, obstructive bronchitis, pulmonary collapse) amplify the danger of carbamazepine-induced respiratory depression and cardiac arrhythmia. The presence of heparin and oxycodone/hydromorphone further complicates the safety profile by raising bleeding and opioid-respiratory depression risks.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 256, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29802992-Buspirone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 28 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with advanced age (82 years). Buspirone is predominantly eliminated unchanged by the kidneys; in acute kidney failure, its clearance is markedly impaired, leading to drug accumulation and increased risk of central nervous system toxicity (e.g., dizziness, confusion, sedation). Additionally, the patient's polypharmacy (17 concurrent medications) raises concerns for drug-drug interactions, particularly with paroxetine (both are serotonergic, increasing serotonin syndrome risk) and with ranitidine (which can alter buspirone metabolism via CYP2D6 inhibition). The presence of hyponatremia and encephalopathy further heightens the vulnerability to CNS adverse effects. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 257, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27882036-Enoxaparin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (92 years) combined with moderate renal impairment (eGFR 53 mL/min/1.73m2) and a high number of concurrent medications (14 listed). Enoxaparin, a low-molecular-weight heparin, carries a risk of excessive bleeding, and the patient's polypharmacy, renal dysfunction, and age-related physiological decline create a synergistic bleeding risk that overrides any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 258, "uid": "mimic-ddi-26706939-Ciprofloxacin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's current hypokalemia (K+ 2.7 mEq/L) combined with the addition of ciprofloxacin, a fluoroquinolone known to cause dose-dependent hypokalemia. This creates a drug-drug interaction that can precipitate life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias (e.g., torsades de pointes) and worsen the underlying syncope/collapse. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (83 years) and multiple comorbidities (cerebral aneurysm, rib fractures, collapsed vertebra, UTI, hypertension) further amplify vulnerability to adverse effects from ciprofloxacin, including QT prolongation, neuromuscular blockade, and falls.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 259, "uid": "mimic-safe-28477357-Pantoprazole", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute alcoholic hepatitis (hepatic impairment) and concurrent use of hydromorphone (IV) and pantoprazole (IV) create a hazard. Pantoprazole is primarily metabolized by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4; hydromorphone is a CYP3A4 substrate. In acute alcoholic hepatitis, CYP3A4 activity is reduced, increasing hydromorphone exposure and risk of respiratory depression. Additionally, both drugs are cleared renally, and combined use may elevate systemic toxicity. The patient's pneumonia and pulmonary collapse further heighten the danger of respiratory compromise.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 260, "uid": "mimic-safe-20192635-Fluticasonetopical", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 35 mL/min/1.73m2) and poisoning by insulins and antidiabetic agents, combined with the ordered topical fluticasone. The key conflict is the potential for fluticasone to mask or worsen the patient's already compromised renal function and the risk of additive hypoglycemia from insulin and antidiabetic agents.\\n\\n2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 261, "uid": "mimic-safe-23831430-Amantadine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes multiple agents with known QT-prolonging potential (fentanyl, lorazepam, zolpidem, famotidine, piperacillin, vancomycin, magnesium sulfate, potassium chloride, insulin, heparin, and amantadine itself). The addition of amantadine to this polypharmacy creates a high-risk additive QT-interval prolongation hazard, especially in the setting of acute respiratory failure, septicemia, and electrolyte disturbances (hyperkalemia, hypernatremia, hyperosmolality). The patient's mild renal impairment (eGFR 88) does not significantly alter amantadine clearance, but the cumulative proarrhythmic effect overrides any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 262, "uid": "mimic-safe-25563031-Cephalexin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes multiple central nervous system depressants (lorazepam, hydroxyzine, oxycodone, hydromorphone, propranolol, methylprednisolone) and an epinephrine order. Cephalexin, a cephalosporin antibiotic, can cause dose-dependent neuromuscular blockade and may potentiate CNS depression. The concurrent use of these agents creates a high risk of additive sedation, respiratory depression, and cardiovascular instability. Additionally, the patient's asthma (unspecified type) and other respiratory impairment raise concern for potential bronchospasm or exacerbation with cephalosporin-induced histamine release. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 263, "uid": "mimic-safe-26048429-Bupivacaine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes multiple agents that directly antagonize the cardiovascular and neuromuscular effects of bupivacaine, creating a hazard that overrides any general safety assumptions. The specific drugs are: (1) **Heparin** (5000 units SC, twice daily) - anticoagulant that increases bleeding risk; (2) **Vancomycin** (1000 mg IV) - known to prolong the QT interval; (3) **Magnesium sulfate** (2 g IV) - can depress cardiac conduction and potentiate bupivacaine's cardiotoxicity; (4) **Potassium chloride** (60 mEq IV) and **Potassium phosphate** (15 mmol IV) - hyperkalemia may exacerbate arrhythmia risk; (5) **Hydromorphone** (0.24 mg IVPCA) - opioid co-administration can mask signs of toxicity and may cause respiratory depression when combined with bupivacaine's potential for hypotension. These concurrent drugs create a polypharmacy hazard that makes the bupivacaine order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 264, "uid": "mimic-safe-24656677-Magnesiumsulfate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **moderately reduced renal function (eGFR 41 mL/min/1.73m2)** combined with **advanced age (81 years)**. Magnesium sulfate is predominantly eliminated renally; in this setting, accumulation can precipitate hypermagnesemia, which is particularly dangerous in elderly patients with compromised renal reserve. Additionally, the patient's **uncontrolled diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)** may alter magnesium homeostasis, further increasing susceptibility to toxicity.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 265, "uid": "mimic-safe-28479513-Latanoprost", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's subdural hemorrhage without an open intracranial wound. Latanoprost, a prostaglandin F2\u03b1 analog, increases aqueous humor outflow and can cause intraocular pressure fluctuations. In the setting of a subdural hematoma, even minor changes in intraocular pressure may alter cerebrospinal fluid dynamics, potentially exacerbating intracranial hypertension or displacing the clot. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (83 years) and high BMI (30.9) further amplify the risk of adverse ocular effects and systemic complications.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 266, "uid": "mimic-ddi-21255400-Haloperidol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of **warfarin** (a CYP2C9 substrate) and the ordered **haloperidol** (a moderate CYP2C9 inhibitor). This drug-drug interaction elevates the risk of supratherapeutic INR and bleeding, especially in a patient with coronary atherosclerosis and prior hemorrhage complicating a procedure. Additionally, the patient's age (78 years) and frailty further amplify the risk of adverse effects from haloperidol, such as QT prolongation, orthostatic hypotension, and neuroleptic malignant syndrome.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 267, "uid": "mimic-safe-28157142-Finasteride", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 32 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with advanced age (87 years). Finasteride is contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment because its active metabolite, 7\u03b1-dihydrotestosterone, accumulates, increasing the risk of reversible priapism and other adverse effects. Additionally, the patient's nonrheumatic aortic stenosis and acute-on-chronic systolic heart failure create a hemodynamic vulnerability that could be exacerbated by any drug-induced hypotension or metabolic disturbance.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 268, "uid": "mimic-age-28003918-Paroxetine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's renal impairment (eGFR 56 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of paroxetine with atenolol and lisinopril. Paroxetine is a potent CYP2D6 inhibitor, which can elevate atenolol levels (a CYP2D6 substrate), increasing risk of bradycardia, hypotension, and heart block. Additionally, paroxetine's serotonergic activity may potentiate the antihypertensive effects of lisinopril, further lowering blood pressure. The patient's advanced age (65 years) and obesity (BMI 39.1) compound vulnerability to these interactions. The ordered dose of 40 mg is high for a 3-day course, and the renal impairment reduces paroxetine clearance, raising the likelihood of accumulation and toxicity.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 269, "uid": "mimic-safe-24982426-Calciumgluconate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent administration of lidocaine (a class IB antiarrhythmic) and the ordered calcium gluconate. The patient's NSTEMI myocardial infarction, cardiogenic shock, and heart failure create a vulnerable cardiac substrate. The specific drug-drug interaction between lidocaine and calcium gluconate is the decisive factor.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 270, "uid": "mimic-safe-23983182-Lidocaine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (80 years) combined with chronic systolic and diastolic heart failure, coronary atherosclerosis, and the concurrent use of heparin (anticoagulant) and alteplase (thrombolytic). Lidocaine, a class Ia antiarrhythmic, can prolong the QT interval and lower the seizure threshold, while heparin and alteplase increase bleeding risk. In a patient with compromised cardiac function, lidocaine's proarrhythmic potential is amplified, and the additive bleeding risk from anticoagulation and thrombolytic therapy makes the combination unsafe.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 271, "uid": "mimic-safe-25172300-Febuxostat", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 29 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent use of oxycodone (a mu-opioid agonist) and irbesartan (an ARB). Febuxostat, a xanthine oxidase inhibitor, is contraindicated in severe renal impairment (eGFR <30) due to accumulation of its active metabolite, oxonic acid, which can precipitate acute interstitial nephritis. Additionally, the patient's atrial fibrillation and congestive heart failure create a proarrhythmic substrate, and the addition of a new drug with potential QT-prolonging effects (though not directly stated) amplifies cardiac risk. The concurrent use of oxycodone and irbesartan further elevates the risk of hypotension and respiratory depression, especially in a patient with compromised renal function and underlying cardiovascular disease.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 272, "uid": "mimic-ddi-21133938-Warfarin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 40), acute hepatic failure (ALT/AST >400, INR 1.2), cardiogenic shock, and concurrent use of multiple interacting drugs (aspirin, heparin, digoxin, hydralazine, isosorbide dinitrate, senna) create a high-risk scenario for warfarin therapy. The combination of reduced renal and hepatic clearance, altered drug metabolism, and additive anticoagulant/antiplatelet effects dramatically elevates bleeding hazard.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 273, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24256866-Spironolactone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **acute kidney failure (eGFR 57 mL/min/1.73m2)** combined with **current potassium chloride therapy**. Spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic, directly antagonizes aldosterone and can cause hyperkalemia. In a patient with renal impairment, the potassium excretion reserve is already diminished, and concurrent potassium supplementation amplifies the risk of life-threatening hyperkalemia.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 274, "uid": "mimic-safe-22942076-Sodiumbicarbonate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73m2) and hyperkalemia (6.3 mEq/L) create a direct hazard with sodium bicarbonate. The drug's dose (50 mEq IV) exceeds safe limits in this setting, risking volume overload, metabolic alkalosis, and further elevation of serum potassium, which can precipitate cardiac arrest. Additionally, the patient's hepatic encephalopathy and ongoing use of multiple vasoactive/inotropic agents (e.g., norepinephrine, vasopressin) amplify the risk of electrolyte-driven arrhythmias and hemodynamic instability.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 275, "uid": "mimic-age-29842315-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 9 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (89 years) create a critical hazard for lorazepam. The drug's active metabolite, nordazepam, accumulates in renal failure, prolonging sedation and respiratory depression. Additionally, the patient is on multiple CNS-active medications (methadone, hydromorphone, lidocaine, hyoscyamine, glycopyrronium, ondansetron) that synergistically depress the central nervous system and respiratory drive. The presence of respiratory failure and diastolic heart failure further compromises compensatory mechanisms, making even a low lorazepam dose dangerous.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 276, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22251969-Warfarin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 12 mL/min/1.73m2) is the primary hazard. Warfarin is hepatically metabolized, but its anticoagulant effect is prolonged in renal failure due to reduced clearance of its active metabolites and accumulation of vitamin K antagonists. Additionally, the patient is on multiple interacting drugs (e.g., oxycodone, lorazepam, miconazole, bupropion, carvedilol, insulin, salbutamol, fluticasone, senna, docusate, iron, levothyroxine, methylprednisolone, glucagon, ezetimibe, febuxostat, ondansetron, acetylsalicylic acid) that can independently alter warfarin metabolism or increase bleeding risk. The combination of severe renal impairment and polypharmacy creates a high risk of supratherapeutic INR, hemorrhage, and drug-drug interactions, making the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 277, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27488741-Diazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute-on-chronic diastolic heart failure, chronic passive congestion of the liver, and concurrent use of multiple CNS-active drugs (morphine, quetiapine, hydroxyzine, furosemide, and fluconazole) create a high-risk scenario for benzodiazepine-induced respiratory depression and hepatic metabolism impairment. The ordered diazepam 5 mg PO/NG is unsafe due to additive sedation, potential for respiratory compromise, and altered clearance in the setting of hepatic congestion.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 278, "uid": "mimic-ddi-26275841-Insulinhuman", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's current use of Vecuronium (a non-depolarizing neuromuscular blocker) combined with the ordered insulin human. Vecuronium can cause dose-dependent respiratory depression and prolongation of neuromuscular blockade, while insulin promotes glucose uptake and can exacerbate hypokalemia, which in turn may potentiate Vecuronium's effects. Additionally, the patient's acute respiratory failure and postinflammatory pulmonary fibrosis create a fragile respiratory state that cannot tolerate further respiratory depression. The concurrent use of furosemide (potassium-wasting diuretic) and methylprednisolone (corticosteroid) further increases the risk of hypokalemia, compounding the danger. The presence of acute on chronic diastolic heart failure and coronary atherosclerosis adds cardiovascular instability, making any additional metabolic or electrolyte disturbance dangerous.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 279, "uid": "mimic-ddi-20437651-Tramadol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's concurrent use of multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants and anticoagulants, which synergistically increase the risk of respiratory depression and bleeding when combined with tramadol.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 280, "uid": "mimic-safe-28661809-Docusate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute and chronic respiratory failure with hypoxia, combined with a concurrent acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 46 mL/min/1.73m2), creates a high-risk scenario for docusate administration. Docusate sodium is a surfactant that can cause pulmonary toxicity, including acute respiratory distress syndrome, and is excreted renally. In a patient with compromised renal function and severe hypoxia, the drug's accumulation and adverse effects on the respiratory system override any general safety assumptions, making the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
