{"idx": 282, "uid": "mimic-safe-20429160-Potassiumphosphate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's active hepatic impairment (abscess of liver, acute/subacute necrosis) combined with the administration of potassium phosphate intravenously. The patient's current medications - ciprofloxacin, metronidazole, and hydromorphone - further compound the risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 283, "uid": "mimic-age-26703331-Clonazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute respiratory failure with hypoxia (eGFR 78, mild renal impairment). Clonazepam, a benzodiazepine, depresses the central nervous system and can further suppress respiratory drive, directly worsening hypoxia. Additionally, the patient is currently on ciprofloxacin (a moderate CYP3A4 inhibitor) and hydromorphone (a CYP3A4 substrate), creating a drug-drug interaction that elevates clonazepam exposure and increases sedation risk. The combination of respiratory impairment, age, and interacting medications overrides any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 284, "uid": "mimic-safe-27487226-Docusate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile is dominated by severe renal impairment (eGFR 39 mL/min/1.73m2, acute kidney failure) and a complex polypharmacy regimen. The ordered docusate (100 mg, 2 times/day) is a non-absorbed stool softener, but its safety is compromised by the patient's concurrent use of multiple drugs that can cause or worsen electrolyte disturbances, particularly hyperkalemia (current potassium 5.9 mEq/L). The combination of renal impairment, hyperkalemia, and polypharmacy creates a high-risk scenario for drug-drug interactions and electrolyte instability, overriding any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 285, "uid": "mimic-safe-24420677-Finasteride", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute-on-chronic systolic heart failure (congestive) combined with severe chronic kidney disease (eGFR 36 mL/min/1.73m2). Finasteride, a 5\u03b1-reductase inhibitor, is contraindicated in patients with active heart failure because it can cause fluid retention, edema, and exacerbate cardiac workload. Additionally, the patient's hypovolemic shock and pleural effusion indicate volume depletion, which further heightens the risk of worsening heart failure with any agent that promotes fluid retention. The renal impairment (stage 4) also raises concern for accumulation of finasteride metabolites, though the primary driver is the cardiac contraindication.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 286, "uid": "mimic-age-20437651-Ketorolac", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of multiple drugs with known interactions with ketorolac, combined with the presence of atrial fibrillation and hyperpotassemia. Specifically, the co-administration of ketorolac with ibuprofen, acetaminophen, and hydromorphone creates a risk of additive nephrotoxicity and bleeding diathesis. Additionally, the patient's atrial fibrillation and hyperpotassemia are exacerbated by NSAID-mediated renal impairment and electrolyte shifts, while the concurrent use of carbamazepine and lamotrigine with ketorolac may alter drug metabolism and increase seizure risk. The traumatic pneumothorax further complicates respiratory safety, as ketorolac can cause bronchospasm or mask respiratory distress.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 287, "uid": "mimic-safe-22059910-Nicotine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of carbamazepine (500 mg BID) and lamotrigine (300 mg daily) with the ordered nicotine creates a hazardous drug-drug interaction. Carbamazepine is a potent inducer of CYP3A4 and CYP2C19, while lamotrigine is a CYP3A4/2C19 substrate. Nicotine is primarily metabolized by CYP1A2, but its metabolism can be influenced by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4. The induction effect of carbamazepine accelerates nicotine clearance, potentially reducing its therapeutic effect, while lamotrigine levels may be altered, increasing the risk of toxicity. Additionally, the patient's atrial fibrillation and acute respiratory failure heighten the cardiovascular and respiratory risks of nicotine use, especially given his current use of oxycodone and heparin.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 288, "uid": "mimic-safe-29176490-Simethicone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### 1. CLINICAL ASSESSMENT\\nThe patient's profile - advanced age (77 years), coronary atherosclerosis, and concurrent use of multiple cardiovascular and antiplatelet medications (atorvastatin, citalopram, clopidogrel, nitroglycerin, ASA, atropine, trazodone) - creates a complex pharmacodynamic milieu. The addition of simethicone, though generally inert, introduces a theoretical risk of altered absorption or interaction with these agents, particularly those affecting gastrointestinal motility or platelet function.\\n\\n### 2. PHARMACOLOGICAL BASIS\\nSimethicone is a non-absorbable gas-liquid separator that may delay gastric emptying or alter the dissolution rate of co-administered drugs. In patients on clopidogrel (a P-gp/CYP3A4 substrate) and ASA (a COX-1 inhibitor), even minor changes in absorption could theoretically affect antiplatelet efficacy or increase bleeding risk. Additionally, simethicone's potential to slow gastric transit might prolong the exposure of citalopram (CYP2C19 substrate) or trazodone (CYP3A4 substrate), raising the risk of QT-interval prolongation or other adverse effects.\\n\\n### 3. PATIENT-SPECIFIC ANALYSIS\\nWhile simethicone is typically considered safe, the patient's polypharmacy, age-related physiological decline, and underlying coronary disease override general safety assumptions. The potential for drug-drug interaction, even if minor, in a vulnerable elderly patient with multiple comorbidities and concurrent medications warrants caution. Thus, the verdict of \\\"unsafe\\\" is justified.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 289, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29858644-Potassiumchloride", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute-on-chronic systolic heart failure (cardiac impairment) combined with moderate renal impairment (eGFR 41 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of furosemide (loop diuretic) and spironolactone (potassium-sparing diuretic). Adding potassium chloride directly to this profile creates a high risk of hyperkalemia, which can precipitate life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 290, "uid": "mimic-safe-25239799-Iron", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of **Acetylsalicylic acid (ASA)** (325 mg daily) and the newly ordered **Iron (325 mg daily)**. The specific attribute is the patient's **chronic condition of aortic valve disorders** (which may predispose to bleeding) and the **current medication ASA**, a potent antiplatelet agent. The addition of iron creates a drug-drug interaction that can impair ASA absorption and increase gastrointestinal bleeding risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 291, "uid": "mimic-renal-28872262-Spironolactone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk", "Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 30 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent use of furosemide (loop diuretic) and lisinopril (ACE inhibitor). Spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic, adds to the risk of hyperkalemia. Additionally, the patient's hyponatremia and hypotension further destabilize electrolyte and hemodynamic balance.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 292, "uid": "mimic-safe-22059910-Salbutamol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### 1. CLINICAL ASSESSMENT\\nThe patient's concurrent use of **carbamazepine** (a potent CYP3A4 inducer) and **oxycodone** (a CYP3A4 substrate) creates a drug-drug interaction hazard. Additionally, **heparin** (anticoagulant) and **oxycodone** (opioid) together elevate bleeding risk due to additive effects on hemostasis.\\n\\n### 2.\\n**PHARMACOLOGICAL BASIS**\\nCarbamazepine induces CYP3A4, accelerating oxycodone metabolism and reducing its analgesic efficacy. Heparin increases bleeding risk, and oxycodone can cause respiratory depression, which is especially dangerous in a patient with acute respiratory failure and pulmonary embolism.\\n\\n### 3.\\n**PATIENT-SPECIFIC ANALYSIS**\\nWhile salbutamol is generally safe, the patient's polypharmacy and underlying respiratory compromise override general safety assumptions. The interaction with carbamazepine and heparin makes the ordered regimen unsafe, necessitating the available evidence *Unsafe* verdict.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 293, "uid": "mimic-ddi-25384176-Carvedilol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **moderately reduced renal function (eGFR 31 mL/min/1.73m2)** combined with **acute-on-chronic diastolic heart failure** and **atrial fibrillation**. The recommended carvedilol dose (25 mg twice daily) is standard for heart failure, but the patient's renal impairment and pre-existing cardiac compromise create a high risk of excessive bradycardia, hypotension, and worsening heart failure. Additionally, the patient is currently on **oxycodone** (opioid) and **salbutamol** (beta-agonist), which can interact with carvedilol's beta-blocking and alpha-blocking properties, further destabilizing cardiac rhythm and blood pressure.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 294, "uid": "mimic-ddi-20437651-Acetylsalicylicacid", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of multiple medications with known pharmacodynamic and pharmacokinetic interactions with acetylsalicylic acid (ASA). Specifically, the combination of ASA with ibuprofen (NSAID), oxycodone, hydromorphone, carbamazepine, lamotrigine, and heparin creates a multifaceted risk profile that overrides any general safety assumptions for low-dose ASA.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 295, "uid": "mimic-age-24540843-Scopolamine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 21 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (79 years) create a high-risk scenario for scopolamine accumulation. Additionally, the patient is currently receiving multiple CNS depressants (morphine, fentanyl, lorazepam, haloperidol, ondansetron, glycopyrronium) that synergistically depress the central nervous system and respiratory drive. The ordered scopolamine dose (1 PTCH, 0 times/day, TD) is not adjusted for renal impairment, and the combination of anticholinergic, sedative, and respiratory-depressant agents overrides any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 296, "uid": "mimic-ddi-25973915-Linezolid", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (80 years) combined with linezolid's known adverse effects of serotonin syndrome and optic neuropathy. Additionally, the patient's acute-on-chronic diastolic heart failure with paroxysmal ventricular tachycardia creates a proarrhythmic vulnerability that linezolid can exacerbate. The concurrent use of metoprolol (beta-blocker) and enalaprilat (ACE inhibitor) further heightens the risk of bradycardia and hypotension when linezolid is administered.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 297, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28662225-Fentanyl", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's advanced age (81 years) and the concurrent use of multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants. The ordered fentanyl, a potent opioid, will add to the cumulative sedative burden, increasing the risk of respiratory depression, excessive sedation, and falls. The patient's septicemia and acute respiratory failure further amplify vulnerability.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 298, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27672872-Simvastatin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (84 years) combined with a low body weight (53.7 kg) and mild renal impairment (eGFR 74 mL/min/1.73m2). These attributes create a high-risk profile for simvastatin accumulation and myopathy, especially when co-administered with amlodipine (a CYP3A4 substrate) and acetylsalicylic acid (which may potentiate bleeding risk via antiplatelet effect). The patient's cerebral artery occlusion with cerebral infarction further elevates the risk of statin-induced myopathy, which could worsen mobility and recovery.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 299, "uid": "mimic-safe-24490144-Calciumgluconate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of **calcium gluconate** with **potassium chloride** (20 mEq IV) and **neostigmine** (3 mg IV). The specific drug-drug interaction is between calcium gluconate and potassium chloride, which can precipitate **hyperkalemia** and **cardiac arrhythmia**. Additionally, neostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor, may potentiate the cardiac effects of calcium gluconate, further elevating arrhythmia risk.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 300, "uid": "mimic-safe-29488258-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 4 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with hepatic failure. Lorazepam is hepatically metabolized (CYP3A4) and renally excreted as active metabolites (e.g., deslorazepam). In this patient, both clearance pathways are severely impaired, leading to drug accumulation and prolonged sedation. Additionally, the patient's platelet count of 83 K/uL indicates coagulopathy, which may be exacerbated by lorazepam's potential to impair platelet function or mask bleeding risk. The concurrent use of multiple interacting medications (sucralfate, lactulose, rifaximin, midodrine, levothyroxine, allopurinol, ipratropium) further complicates safe dosing.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 301, "uid": "mimic-renal-24244087-Spironolactone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced eGFR (22 mL/min/1.73m2) from acute kidney failure. Spironolactone is contraindicated in acute kidney injury because it blocks the distal tubular potassium-secreting mechanism, leading to hyperkalemia. The patient's concurrent use of ACE inhibitors (implied by lisinopril-like effect from heart failure therapy) and potassium-sparing agents (spironolactone) creates a high-risk combination for life-threatening hyperkalemia. Additionally, the patient's chronic passive congestion of the liver (hepatic impairment) and elevated INR (1.9) suggest coagulopathy, which may be exacerbated by spironolactone's potential to increase INR via CYP3A4 inhibition, though this is secondary to the renal contraindication.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 302, "uid": "mimic-ddi-25563031-Hydromorphone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants (lorazepam, hydroxyzine, oxycodone, ketorolac, ondansetron, propranolol, methylprednisolone) and a non-selective beta-blocker (propranolol). Adding hydromorphone, a potent opioid agonist, creates a high-risk pharmacodynamic interaction: additive CNS depression and respiratory depression. Additionally, the patient has asthma, which is a relative contraindication to opioid use due to risk of bronchospasm and respiratory compromise. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 303, "uid": "mimic-safe-26275841-Heparin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes Vecuronium (a non-depolarizing neuromuscular blocker) and Fentanyl (an opioid analgesic). Heparin, when administered with Vecuronium, can potentiate the neuromuscular blockade, leading to exaggerated respiratory depression. Additionally, Fentanyl and Heparin both depress the central nervous system; their combined use increases the risk of profound sedation, hypoventilation, and respiratory failure. The patient's acute respiratory failure and postinflammatory pulmonary fibrosis further amplify this hazard. The concurrent use of multiple respiratory depressants (Vecuronium, Fentanyl, Propofol, Midazolam) with Heparin creates a multiplicative risk of respiratory compromise, overriding any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 304, "uid": "mimic-ddi-23831430-Haloperidol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute respiratory failure, septicemia, and multiple electrolyte disturbances (hyperosmolality, hypernatremia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia) create a high-risk environment for haloperidol use. The drug's known propensity to cause QT prolongation and torsades de pointes is amplified by the existing electrolyte imbalances and concurrent medications (e.g., fentanyl, lidocaine, insulin, heparin) that further destabilize cardiac repolarization. Additionally, the patient's portal hypertension and hepatic impairment (ALT/AST elevation) may reduce haloperidol clearance, increasing systemic exposure and toxicity risk.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
