{"idx": 306, "uid": "mimic-safe-28861356-Glucagon", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **congestive heart failure (CHF)** combined with **advanced age (80 years)** and **multiple interacting medications**. Glucagon is a cardiac stimulant that increases heart rate and contractility, which can precipitate arrhythmias or decompensate in CHF. Additionally, the patient's ileostomy and ileus risk from glucagon's pro-motility effect create a gastrointestinal hazard. The concurrent use of metoprolol (beta-blocker) and piperacillin (which can cause electrolyte disturbances) further complicates safety.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 307, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28861356-Fluconazole", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **moderately reduced renal function (eGFR 56 mL/min/1.73m2)** combined with the **concurrent use of metoprolol (5 mg IV)**. Fluconazole is a potent inhibitor of CYP2D6 and a moderate inhibitor of CYP3A4, which can elevate metoprolol concentrations, increasing the risk of bradycardia, hypotension, and other beta-blocker toxicity. Additionally, the patient's **ileostomy** creates a high-risk environment for fungal dissemination if systemic antifungal therapy is required, and the **congestive heart failure** further complicates management. The **age of 80 years** amplifies susceptibility to adverse drug reactions and renal impairment progression.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 308, "uid": "mimic-age-24717014-Nifedipine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's concurrent use of **omeprazole** (a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor) with **nifedipine** (a CYP3A4 substrate). This combination creates a clinically significant drug-drug interaction that elevates nifedipine exposure, increasing the risk of hypotension, reflex tachycardia, and potential cardiac ischemia. Additionally, the patient's **age (67 years)** and **hypertensive chronic kidney disease** further amplify vulnerability to adverse cardiovascular effects. The prescribed dose of 90 mg once daily is at the upper end of the therapeutic range, which is especially dangerous when combined with a CYP3A4 inhibitor.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 309, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28662225-Tramadol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's septicemia due to anaerobes combined with acute respiratory failure and septic shock. Tramadol, a serotonergic opioid, can precipitate serotonin syndrome when co-administered with multiple serotonergic agents present in her current medication list (e.g., vancomycin, metronidazole, levofloxacin, omeprazole, senna, acetaminophen, magnesium sulfate). Additionally, tramadol's respiratory-depressant effect is amplified in the setting of acute respiratory failure, and its anticholinergic properties may worsen her already compromised respiratory status. The patient's advanced age (81 years) further reduces drug clearance and increases sensitivity to adverse effects.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 310, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27876215-Methylprednisolone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's severely reduced eGFR (24 mL/min/1.73m2) and elevated INR (3.5). Methylprednisolone, a glucocorticoid, can induce hyperglycemia, increase infection risk, and may exacerbate hypertension, all of which are particularly dangerous in a patient with heart failure, chronic kidney disease, and atrial fibrillation. The concurrent use of oxycodone (an opioid) with a corticosteroid raises the risk of additive respiratory depression and sedation. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (68 years) and obesity (BMI 30.3) further amplify these risks.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 311, "uid": "mimic-safe-29176490-Latanoprost", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - particularly clopidogrel, aspirin, and nitroglycerin - create a hazard when combined with latanoprost. Latanoprost, a prostaglandin F2\u03b1 agonist, can cause intraocular pressure elevation and systemic vasodilation. In a patient with coronary atherosclerosis and unstable angina, systemic vasodilation may exacerbate hypotension and reduce coronary perfusion pressure, while the antiplatelet effect of aspirin and clopidogrel increases bleeding risk. The concurrent use of nitroglycerin (a venodilator) further amplifies the risk of severe hypotension. These factors collectively override any ocular benefit, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
