{"idx": 313, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28613200-Ondansetron", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's extensive polypharmacy - including morphine, oxycodone, morphine, diphenhydramine, trazodone, ciprofloxacin, and other CNS-active agents - creates a high-risk pharmacodynamic profile. Adding ondansetron, a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist with known QT-prolonging potential, to this regimen substantially elevates the risk of additive QT-interval prolongation and torsades de pointes. Additionally, the patient's diabetes with neurological manifestations and foot ulceration, combined with current insulin therapy, introduces a risk of hypoglycemia if ondansetron's potential to delay gastric emptying or alter glucose metabolism is clinically significant. The concurrent use of ciprofloxacin (a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor) with ondansetron (a CYP3A4 substrate) further raises the risk of ondansetron toxicity. These overlapping hazards override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 314, "uid": "mimic-safe-23721604-Oseltamivir", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's renal impairment (eGFR 56 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (82 years) create a hazard for oseltamivir accumulation. Additionally, the concurrent use of multiple interacting drugs (e.g., omeprazole, paroxetine, propranolol, atorvastatin, amlodipine, vancomycin, insulin, heparin) elevates the risk of altered oseltamivir pharmacokinetics and potential toxicity. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 315, "uid": "mimic-safe-20626031-Magnesiumsulfate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes Potassium chloride (40 mEq once daily PO) and Heparin (25,000 units IV). Magnesium sulfate is a known potassium-depleting agent and can also potentiate the anticoagulant effect of heparin. The patient's potassium level is already critically low at 2.9 mEq/L, placing him at high risk for hypokalemia-induced cardiac arrhythmias. The combination of magnesium sulfate with potassium chloride and heparin creates a dual hazard: worsening hypokalemia and increased bleeding risk. Additionally, the patient's atrial fibrillation and atrial flutter, combined with congestive heart failure, make him vulnerable to magnesium-induced bradycardia or arrhythmia. These factors override any general safety assumptions, rendering the magnesium sulfate order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 316, "uid": "mimic-safe-24717014-Mupirocin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### 1. CLINICAL ASSESSMENT\\nThe patient's chronic kidney disease (eGFR 66 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of multiple interacting medications (nifedipine, omeprazole, ASA, heparin, metoprolol, docusate, gabapentin) create a hazard when combined with topical mupirocin. The drug's systemic absorption, though minimal, can alter the pharmacokinetics of co-administered agents and increase bleeding risk via heparin interaction.\\n\\n### 2. PHARMACOLOGICAL BASIS\\nMupirocin is a topical antibiotic that can be absorbed into the bloodstream, potentially inhibiting CYP3A4 (nifedipine, metoprolol) and CYP2C19 (omeprazole, ASA), leading to unpredictable drug levels. Additionally, mupirocin may potentiate the anticoagulant effect of heparin, raising the risk of bleeding.\\n\\n### 3. PATIENT-SPECIFIC ANALYSIS\\nGeneral safety assumptions for topical mupirocin (low systemic absorption) are overridden by the patient's polypharmacy and mild renal impairment, which together amplify the potential for drug-drug interactions and bleeding complications, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 317, "uid": "mimic-age-22756440-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (87 years) combined with subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) without an open intracranial wound, and the concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and cardiovascular medications. Lorazepam, a benzodiazepine, is contraindicated in SAH due to risk of increased intracranial pressure and respiratory depression. Additionally, the patient's polypharmacy (ondansetron, sertraline, mirtazapine, metoprolol, amiodarone, hydralazine, etc.) creates a high risk of additive CNS depression and cardiac instability. The moderate renal impairment (eGFR 59) further reduces clearance of lorazepam metabolites, compounding toxicity.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 318, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28861356-Fentanyl", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's ileostomy with disrupted wound, advanced age (80 years), moderate renal impairment (eGFR 56), and concurrent use of multiple interacting medications (metoprolol, acetaminophen, heparin, ondansetron, ipratropium, pantoprazole). Fentanyl is a potent opioid that can cause respiratory depression, sedation, and hemodynamic instability, which are especially dangerous in a patient with ileostomy-related fluid/electrolyte disturbances, reduced renal clearance, and a high-risk medication profile.\\n\\n2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 319, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28889419-Promethazine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile is dominated by a severe thoracic aortic injury, which creates a high-risk hemodynamic environment. The ordered promethazine (6.25 mg IV) is a phenothiazine antihistamine that possesses significant anticholinergic and sedative properties. In the setting of aortic injury, any drug that can cause hypotension, tachycardia, or altered mental status can destabilize the fragile aortic wall and increase the risk of rupture or hemorrhage. Additionally, the patient is currently receiving multiple vasodilators (nitroglycerin, hydralazine, esmolol, fentanyl, hydromorphone) and sedatives (midazolam, ketorolac, ondansetron), which collectively lower blood pressure and depress central nervous system tone. Adding promethazine would further depress the vasomotor center, potentially leading to profound hypotension and loss of compensatory mechanisms, thereby worsening the aortic injury. The patient's asthma also raises concern because promethazine can cause bronchospasm, which would be particularly dangerous in a patient with compromised respiratory reserve. Thus, the combination of aortic injury, concurrent vasodilator/sedative therapy, and asthma makes promethazine unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 320, "uid": "mimic-ddi-25384176-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's moderate renal impairment (eGFR 31 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with the use of oxycodone, a mu-opioid agonist that undergoes extensive hepatic metabolism but also undergoes renal elimination of its active metabolite (normeperidone). In the setting of acute-on-chronic diastolic heart failure, the drug's potential to cause respiratory depression, hypotension, and sedation is amplified by the patient's compromised cardiac function and fluid status. Additionally, the patient is currently on carvedilol (a beta-blocker with negative inotropic effects) and salbutamol (a beta-agonist), creating a potential for hemodynamic instability if opioid-induced hypotension occurs. The concurrent use of bupropion (a CYP2B6 inhibitor) could theoretically increase oxycodone exposure, though the clinical significance is uncertain; however, the renal impairment remains the decisive factor.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 321, "uid": "mimic-safe-27996267-Ezetimibe", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 30 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent use of ciprofloxacin (a moderate CYP3A4 inhibitor) and digoxin (a narrow therapeutic index drug). Ezetimibe, while generally safe, can modestly inhibit CYP3A4, and the renal impairment reduces digoxin clearance, creating a synergistic risk of digoxin toxicity. Additionally, the patient's congestive heart failure and diuretic-like action of ezetimibe may exacerbate volume depletion in a setting of acute kidney injury.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 322, "uid": "mimic-age-28477280-Metoclopramide", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of metronidazole (500 mg IV) and metoclopramide (10 mg IV) creates a hazardous drug-drug interaction. Metoclopramide is a dopamine D2 receptor antagonist that can block the metabolism of metronidazole, increasing its systemic exposure and risk of neurotoxicity (e.g., peripheral neuropathy, ataxia). Additionally, the patient's malignant intrahepatic bile duct neoplasm (cholangiocarcinoma) is associated with a high incidence of portal hypertension and variceal bleeding; metoclopramide's prokinetic effect can exacerbate portal pressure and precipitate variceal hemorrhage. The patient's age (67 years) further compounds vulnerability to adverse effects. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 323, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22251969-Ondansetron", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 12 mL/min/1.73m2). Ondansetron is predominantly eliminated renally; in severe renal impairment, its clearance is markedly reduced, leading to drug accumulation and increased risk of QT prolongation, torsades de pointes, and neurotoxicity. Additionally, the patient is concurrently on warfarin, oxycodone, and multiple other medications, creating a complex polypharmacy profile that further elevates the risk of adverse drug interactions and bleeding.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 324, "uid": "mimic-ddi-25864431-Diazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severe hepatic impairment (hepatic failure without coma) combined with advanced renal impairment (eGFR 10 mL/min/1.73m2). Diazepam is hepatically metabolized (CYP2C19, CYP3A4) and its active metabolite, nordiazepam, accumulates in both liver and kidney failure. The patient's concurrent use of hydromorphone (a CYP3A4 substrate) and heparin (which may potentiate CNS depression) further amplifies the risk of excessive sedation, respiratory depression, and coagulopathy. The ordered dose (10 mg) is standard for a normal adult, but in this setting it is excessive due to reduced clearance, and the duration (3 days) increases cumulative toxicity risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 325, "uid": "mimic-safe-28166872-Acetaminophen", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute respiratory failure, methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus pneumonia, and asthma create a high-risk respiratory milieu. Acetaminophen, while generally safe, can cause hepatotoxicity and may mask fever, delaying infection control. The concurrent use of multiple CNS depressants (fentanyl, midazolam, propofol, lorazepam, morphine, hydralazine, levetiracetam, phenylephrine) and the need for prophylactic influenza vaccination further complicate safety. The patient's advanced age (71 years) and weight (56 kg) increase vulnerability to adverse drug reactions and reduced clearance of interacting agents. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 326, "uid": "mimic-safe-20626031-Docusate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - morphine, furosemide, metoprolol, and potassium chloride - create a hazard when combined with docusate. Docusate is a stool softener that can reduce gastrointestinal motility, potentially delaying the absorption of orally administered drugs and increasing the risk of drug accumulation. Additionally, docusate may alter the absorption of furosemide, metoprolol, and potassium chloride, leading to unpredictable pharmacokinetics. The patient's congestive heart failure and atrial fibrillation further amplify the need for stable drug levels, as any fluctuation in electrolyte balance or beta-blocker effect can precipitate arrhythmia or hemodynamic instability. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, rendering the docusate order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 327, "uid": "mimic-age-29974575-Clonazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile contains multiple interacting hazards that collectively render clonazepam unsafe. The primary conflicts are: (1) concurrent lidocaine and clonazepam both depress the central nervous system and can synergistically lower the seizure threshold, increasing the risk of excessive sedation and respiratory depression; (2) the patient's acute respiratory failure and pneumonia due to Klebsiella pneumoniae create a fragile respiratory state that cannot tolerate further CNS depression; (3) the presence of septic shock and gram-negative septicemia indicates a hyperdynamic, potentially unstable hemodynamic state, which clonazepam's sedative effects could worsen; (4) the patient is on multiple other medications (fentanyl, heparin, piperacillin, etc.) that each carry their own risks, and the addition of clonazepam introduces a new, unpredictable pharmacodynamic interaction that could destabilize the already complex regimen.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 328, "uid": "mimic-safe-29374560-Hydralazine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of **heparin** (5000 units SC three times daily) and the ordered **hydralazine** (10 mg IV). Heparin is a potent anticoagulant that inhibits thrombin and factor Xa, while hydralazine is a direct vasodilator that can cause reflex tachycardia and increase cardiac output. The combination creates a pharmacodynamic interaction that amplifies bleeding risk and may destabilize hemodynamics in a patient with coronary atherosclerosis of a vein bypass graft, where hemostatic integrity is already compromised.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 329, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24357615-Escitalopram", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and anticoagulant/antiplatelet agents, combined with severe obesity (BMI 36.9) and advanced age (64 years), creates a high-risk scenario for escitalopram. The specific hazards are additive CNS depression, increased bleeding risk from rivaroxaban and antiplatelet agents, and a potential for QTc prolongation given the presence of paroxysmal atrial fibrillation and a history of coronary artery disease. The patient's severe obesity further amplifies the risk of dose-dependent adverse effects and may alter drug distribution and clearance.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 330, "uid": "mimic-safe-25933959-Heparin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of hydralazine (20 mg TID PO/NG) and heparin (5000 units SC BID) creates a clinically significant drug-drug interaction hazard. Hydralazine is a direct vasodilator that can potentiate the anticoagulant effect of heparin, increasing the risk of bleeding. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (68 years) and obesity (BMI 30.3) further amplify susceptibility to heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) and other bleeding complications. The presence of diabetic foot ulcers and chronic kidney disease (CKD) also elevates baseline bleeding risk, making the combination unsafe.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 331, "uid": "mimic-ddi-25085565-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and anticoagulant/antiplatelet agents creates a high-risk pharmacodynamic interaction with lorazepam. Specifically, the combination of oxycodone, morphine, and lorazepam produces additive CNS depression, while the presence of heparin and the patient's uncontrolled diabetes with ketoacidosis and sepsis further amplify the hazard of respiratory depression and metabolic instability.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 332, "uid": "mimic-safe-20755971-Ursodeoxycholicacid", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 72 mL/min/1.73m2) with oliguria/anuria, combined with the ordered use of ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) at a standard dose of 500 mg twice daily. This drug is contraindicated in acute kidney injury because it is primarily eliminated unchanged via the kidneys, and accumulation can precipitate hepatorenal syndrome or worsen renal function. Additionally, the patient's severe hepatic impairment (total bilirubin 17.2 mg/dL, INR 2.5) and coagulation defects further elevate the risk of bleeding, as UDCA may alter platelet function and coagulation pathways. The concurrent use of tramadol (a serotonergic agent) and pantoprazole (a CYP2C19 inhibitor) adds a potential drug-drug interaction risk, though the primary driver is the renal/hepatic contraindication.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 333, "uid": "mimic-safe-28157142-Magnesiumsulfate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 32 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with advanced age (87 years). Magnesium sulfate is predominantly eliminated renally; in acute-on-chronic heart failure with reduced renal function, accumulation can precipitate hypermagnesemia, causing bradycardia, hypotension, and cardiac arrest. Additionally, the patient's nonrheumatic aortic stenosis creates a hemodynamic vulnerability that may be exacerbated by magnesium-induced vasodilation.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 334, "uid": "mimic-ddi-26352758-Hydromorphone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 17 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and GI-motility-altering medications. Hydromorphone, a mu-opioid agonist, is renally cleared and its metabolites accumulate in renal impairment, increasing the risk of respiratory depression, sedation, and altered mental status. The patient's polypharmacy - including lorazepam (benzodiazepine), ondansetron (serotonin antagonist), senna/bisacodyl (laxatives), and insulin - further amplifies CNS depression and gastrointestinal instability, creating a high-risk milieu for opioid-induced adverse events.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 335, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29276678-Hydromorphone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute respiratory failure with hypoxia, which directly contraindicates opioid use. Additionally, the presence of multiple central nervous system depressants (lorazepam, oxycodone, voriconazole) creates a synergistic risk of respiratory depression. The moderate renal impairment (eGFR 55) further compounds the danger by reducing clearance of hydromorphone and its active metabolite, leading to accumulation and prolonged sedation. The patient's low platelet count (30 K/uL) adds bleeding risk, but the immediate safety concern is respiratory compromise.\\n\\n2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 336, "uid": "mimic-safe-27738145-Cefepime", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### 1. CLINICAL ASSESSMENT\\nThe patient's **acute respiratory failure** and **ventilator-associated pneumonia** create a high-risk infection environment. The ordered **cefpime** (a cephalosporin) is not recommended for ventilator-associated pneumonia due to its limited activity against *Pseudomonas aeruginosa* and *Enterobacteriaceae* that are common pathogens in this setting. Additionally, the patient's **malignant neoplasm of the pancreas** and **secondary liver malignancy** may alter drug distribution and clearance, but the primary hazard is the drug-drug interaction with **erythromycin**.\\n\\n### 2. PHARMACOLOGICAL BASIS\\nCefepime is a cephalosparic with a narrow spectrum against Gram-negative rods and Gram-positive cocci. Erythromycin is a macrolide antibiotic that inhibits CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein. Cefepime is a P-glycoprotein substrate; co-administration can reduce its intestinal absorption and systemic exposure, potentially leading to subtherapeutic levels. This interaction is clinically significant and may compromise infection control.\\n\\n### 3. PATIENT-SPECIFIC ANALYSIS\\nWhile cefepime is generally safe in patients with normal renal function and no drug interactions, this patient's specific profile - active ventilator-associated pneumonia requiring broad-spectrum coverage and concurrent erythromycin therapy - overrides general safety assumptions. The combination of a suboptimal antibiotic choice and a drug-drug interaction renders the order unsafe.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 337, "uid": "mimic-safe-27962747-Micafungin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of Heparin (5000 units SC three times daily) and Micafungin creates a drug-drug interaction hazard. Heparin is a potent anticoagulant, and Micafungin can prolong the activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) by inhibiting factor Xa and thrombin. This additive effect elevates the risk of clinically significant bleeding, especially in a patient with a pancreatic cyst/pseudocyst that may already predispose to hemorrhage or coagulopathy.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 338, "uid": "mimic-safe-29488258-Fluticasonetopical", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 4 mL/min/1.73m2) and hepatic failure (INR 1.1, ALT 28/AST 70) create a dual-organ hazard. The ordered fluticasone (topical) is a corticosteroid that undergoes hepatic metabolism and is partially renally excreted. In this setting, accumulation and systemic toxicity are possible. Additionally, the patient is on multiple interacting drugs (sucralfate, heparin, lorazepam, midodrine, lactulose, rifaximin, etc.), which may alter fluticasone absorption or pharmacokinetics. The combination of severe renal and hepatic impairment, polypharmacy, and the need for a short course (2 days) without dose adjustment makes the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 339, "uid": "mimic-age-23403708-Metoclopramide", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (82 years) combined with concurrent use of scopolamine (1 PTCH, once daily, TD) and metoclopramide (10 mg IV). Both agents are anticholinergic, creating a synergistic risk of excessive central nervous system (CNS) and peripheral anticholinergic effects.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 340, "uid": "mimic-safe-28861356-Ipratropium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **congestive heart failure (CHF)** combined with **moderate renal impairment (eGFR 51)** and **current use of metoprolol (beta-blocker) and heparin (anticoagulant)**. The ordered ipratropium, a muscarinic antagonist, can exacerbate CHF by promoting tachycardia and increasing heart rate, counteracting the rate-slowing effect of metoprolol. Additionally, ipratropium may cause tachycardia, which can destabilize a fragile anticoagulated state (heparin) and increase bleeding risk. The ileostomy and ileus risk further compound the need for caution.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 341, "uid": "mimic-safe-27660781-Insulinhuman", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's current medication **Glucagon (1 mg, IM)**, which directly antagonizes the metabolic action of insulin. This creates a pharmacodynamic conflict that overrides any general safety assumptions about insulin use in a patient with a liver abscess.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 342, "uid": "mimic-safe-20297618-Ranitidine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - nitroglycerin, fentanyl, oxycodone, and ranitidine - create a hazard. Ranitidine, an H2-receptor antagonist, can reduce the clearance of fentanyl and oxycodone by inhibiting CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, leading to elevated drug concentrations and increased risk of respiratory depression. Additionally, ranitidine may potentiate the hypotensive effect of nitroglycerin, compounding the patient's already fragile hemodynamic status due to unstable angina and coronary grafts.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 343, "uid": "mimic-safe-24490144-Glucagon", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of multiple vasopressors (phenylephrine, nitroglycerin) and opioids (fentanyl, oxycodone) with the ordered glucagon. Glucagon is a potent alpha-adrenergic agonist that can precipitate severe hypertension and tachycardia, directly opposing the hypotensive and vasodilatory effects of nitroglycerin and phenylephrine. Additionally, glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, increasing serum glucose, which may antagonize the hypoglycemic action of insulin and complicate glycemic control in a patient with atherosclerotic heart disease and NSTEMI. The combination of these opposing hemodynamic and metabolic effects creates a high-risk scenario for cardiovascular instability and metabolic derangement.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 344, "uid": "mimic-safe-21791856-Ezetimibe", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 14 mL/min/1.73m2) and the concurrent use of multiple drugs with known ezetimibe interactions. The renal impairment creates a high risk of ezetimibe accumulation, while the drug-drug interactions (e.g., with metolazone, torasemide, carvedilol, and insulin) can alter cholesterol-lowering efficacy or exacerbate electrolyte disturbances. Additionally, the patient's severe respiratory failure and heart failure further complicate safety.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 345, "uid": "mimic-safe-29374560-Cyanocobalamin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### 1. CLINICAL ASSESSMENT\\nThe patient's concurrent use of **warfarin** (7.5 mg daily) and **heparin** (5000 units SC three times daily) creates a dual anticoagulant regimen. Adding **cyanocobalamin** (vitamin B12) does not directly interact with these agents, but the underlying bleeding risk from the anticoagulants is amplified by the patient's **atrial fibrillation** and **coronary atherosclerosis of autologous vein bypass graft**, which are independent risk factors for hemorrhage. The presence of **hypertensive chronic kidney disease** (eGFR 92 mL/min/1.73m2) is normal, so no renal concern.\\n\\n### 2.\\n### 3.\\n###\\n### 2.\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###\\n###