{"idx": 352, "uid": "mimic-safe-25172300-Ampicillin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 29 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with the high-dose IV ampicillin regimen (3 g q6h). This creates a drug-drug interaction risk with insulin and glucagon, and a toxicity risk from accumulation of ampicillin and its metabolites.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 353, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22741225-Risperidone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's current medication list, which includes multiple agents with known serotonergic activity (fluoxetine, ziprasidone, gabapentin, valproic acid, hydrocodone, senna, simvastatin, fenofibrate, pantoprazole, levothyroxine, insulin, glucagon) and the new risperidone. This creates a high-risk polypharmacy scenario for serotonin syndrome, a potentially life-threatening condition. Additionally, the patient's mild renal impairment (eGFR 69) and obesity (BMI 37.1) further compound the danger by reducing clearance of risperidone and its active metabolite, leading to accumulation and heightened adverse effects.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 354, "uid": "mimic-safe-28477357-Micafungin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes multiple agents that can independently or synergistically cause or worsen hypokalemia (e.g., furosemide, morphine, heparin, alteplase, potassium chloride). The ordered micafungin carries a known risk of hypokalemia, and the patient already has a potassium level of 3.2 mEq/L. This creates a pharmacodynamic drug-drug interaction that amplifies electrolyte depletion, overriding any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 355, "uid": "mimic-safe-29488258-Ipratropium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 4 mL/min/1.73m2) and hepatic failure (INR 1.1, ALT 28/AST 70) create a dual-organ hazard. Ipratropium is metabolized hepatically and its systemic clearance is reduced in severe hepatic impairment, leading to elevated plasma concentrations and increased risk of adverse effects (e.g., excessive bronchodilation, tachycardia, arrhythmia). Additionally, the patient's renal impairment eliminates the possibility of drug elimination via the kidneys, compounding the hepatic risk. The concurrent use of lorazepam (a CNS depressant) further heightens the danger of additive sedation and respiratory depression, especially given the presence of uncomplicated asthma. These overlapping vulnerabilities override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 356, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29462354-Ciprofloxacin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the co-administration of ciprofloxacin with multiple CNS-depressant and anticoagulant agents in a patient with acute myeloid leukemia and pancytopenia. The patient's current medications include oxycodone (opioid), lorazepam (benzodiazepine), dexamethasone (corticosteroid), acyclovir, fluconazole, and others, all of which potentiate ciprofloxacin's central nervous system depression. Additionally, ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP2C9 and CYP3A4, raising fluconazole and acyclovir levels, and may further impair platelet function, exacerbating the existing thrombocytopenia (platelets 28 K/uL). The patient's active infections (bacteremia, viral pneumonia) and compromised hematopoiesis compound the risk of severe adverse events.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 357, "uid": "mimic-age-23403708-Meclizine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's advanced age (82 years) and concurrent use of multiple CNS-active medications (hydromorphone, salbutamol, ondansetron, acetaminophen, famotidine, levothyroxine, furosemide, heparin). Meclizine, a first-generation antihistamine with sedative properties, can further depress the central nervous system. The combination of age-related pharmacodynamic sensitivity and polypharmacy creates a cumulative risk of excessive sedation, respiratory depression, and falls.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 358, "uid": "mimic-ddi-21216581-Diltiazem", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent use of apixaban (a direct factor Xa inhibitor) and diltiazem (a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker). This combination creates a clinically significant drug-drug interaction that elevates bleeding risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 359, "uid": "mimic-renal-28887654-Spironolactone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk", "Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 27 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with concurrent use of potassium chloride (20 mEq twice daily) and digoxin (0.125 mg daily). Spironolactone, a potassium-sparing diuretic, directly antagonizes aldosterone, leading to potassium retention. In this setting, the additive effect of spironolactone and potassium chloride creates a high risk of hyperkalemia, which can precipitate cardiac arrhythmias, including life-threatening digoxin toxicity. Additionally, spironolactone may reduce digoxin clearance via renal excretion, further elevating its serum concentration. The patient's pre-existing cardiomyopathy and heart failure also increase susceptibility to arrhythmias, compounding the danger.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 360, "uid": "mimic-safe-22130791-Aminocaproicacid", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (88 years) create a critical hazard for aminocaproic acid. This drug is primarily renally eliminated; in acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis, accumulation and toxicity are inevitable. Additionally, the patient's thrombocytopenia (84 K/uL) and concurrent use of multiple anticoagulants/vasopressors (e.g., fentanyl, norepinephrine, vasopressin) amplify bleeding risk. The combination of impaired clearance and fragile hemostasis makes the ordered dose unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 361, "uid": "mimic-safe-20214994-Calciumgluconate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's current medication Magnesium sulfate (2 gm IV) and the ordered Calcium gluconate (2 g IV). Magnesium is a potent, non-depolarizing neuromuscular blocker that can cause respiratory depression, hypotension, and cardiac conduction delays. Calcium gluconate is a calcium salt that antagonizes magnesium's neuromuscular effects, but concurrent administration can lead to unpredictable shifts in magnesium and calcium homeostasis, potentially precipitating hypercalcemia or hypomagnesemia. In a patient with acute respiratory failure, any additional respiratory depressant or electrolyte disturbance is dangerous.\\n\\n2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 362, "uid": "mimic-safe-24420677-Tiotropium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's advanced age (87 years), severe chronic kidney disease (eGFR 41 mL/min/1.73m2), acute-on-chronic systolic heart failure, and multiple interacting medications (warfarin, metoprolol, furosemide, hydralazine, insulin, salbutamol, etc.) collectively create a high-risk scenario for tiotropium administration. The primary hazards are additive cardiovascular strain from anticholinergic effects, increased bleeding risk from warfarin and anticholinergic-induced platelet dysfunction, and potential renal impairment exacerbation due to volume depletion from furosemide and hydralazine. These factors override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 363, "uid": "mimic-safe-23831430-Pantoprazole", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure (despite eGFR 100 mL/min/1.73m2 - likely a transient or mislabeled acute injury) and concurrent use of multiple drugs with known interactions with pantoprazole create the hazard. Specifically, the combination of fentanyl, calcium gluconate, potassium phosphate, potassium chloride, and magnesium sulfate with a PPI that reduces gastric pH can precipitate life-threatening arrhythmias (e.g., hyperkalemia, hypermagnesemia, and fentanyl-induced QT prolongation). Additionally, the patient's portal hypertension and hepatic impairment (ALT/AST elevation, INR 1.5) may alter drug metabolism, further elevating risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 364, "uid": "mimic-age-24717014-Meclizine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and anticoagulant/antiplatelet agents (nifedipine, omeprazole, acetylsalicylic acid, gabapentin, oxycodone, zolpidem, diphenhydramine, metoprolol) creates a high baseline risk of additive sedation, respiratory depression, and bleeding. Adding meclizine, a first-generation antihistamine with strong CNS depressant properties, would further potentiate these effects, especially in a patient with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) who may already have compromised respiratory drive. The combination of meclizine with gabapentin, oxycodone, and zolpidem is particularly hazardous due to synergistic CNS depression, which can lead to severe sedation, falls, and respiratory compromise. Additionally, the patient's mild renal impairment (eGFR 82) does not significantly alter meclizine clearance, but the overall polypharmacy profile overrides any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 365, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28477357-Morphine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of hydromorphone (1-2 mg IV) and the newly ordered morphine (15 mg PO TID). Both are mu-opioid agonists, creating a pharmacodynamic drug-drug interaction that elevates the risk of opioid-induced respiratory depression, sedation, and hypotension. Additionally, the patient's chronic pancreatitis with cyst/pseudocyst and acute alcoholic hepatitis may alter opioid metabolism or clearance, further compounding the risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 366, "uid": "mimic-safe-20626031-Potassiumchloride", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of furosemide (a loop diuretic) and morphine (an opioid analgesic). Furosemide promotes potassium excretion, while morphine can cause hypokalemia via direct renal potassium loss and by reducing oral intake. Adding potassium chloride to this regimen creates a high risk of hyperkalemia, especially given the patient's underlying congestive heart failure and atrial fibrillation, which already predispose to electrolyte instability and cardiac arrhythmias.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 367, "uid": "mimic-safe-26275841-Potassiumchloride", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute respiratory failure, postinflammatory pulmonary fibrosis, and concurrent use of Vecuronium (a non-depolarizing neuromuscular blocker) create a high-risk scenario for potassium chloride administration. Vecuronium can cause respiratory depression and muscle weakness, and potassium chloride can exacerbate neuromuscular blockade, leading to life-threatening respiratory compromise. Additionally, the patient's severe obesity (BMI 39.5) and multiple comorbidities (acute on chronic diastolic heart failure, coronary atherosclerosis, diabetes) further amplify the hazard.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 368, "uid": "mimic-safe-28479513-Heparin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's subdural hemorrhage without an open intracranial wound, combined with advanced age (83 years) and moderate renal impairment (eGFR 50 mL/min/1.73m2). Heparin, a potent anticoagulant, would directly increase bleeding risk in this setting. Additionally, the patient's polypharmacy - including multiple antihypertensives (atenolol, amlodipine, metoprolol, labetalol, nicardipine), a diuretic (furosemide not listed but implied via potassium chloride), and a CNS depressant (phenobarbital) - creates a high-risk profile for drug-drug interactions and additive hypotension. The recommended dose (5000 units SC three times daily) is standard for prophylaxis but is inappropriate given the bleeding diathesis and age-related pharmacokinetic changes.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 369, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29462354-Meperidine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **acute myeloid leukemia (AML) without remission** combined with **pancytopenia (platelets 25 K/uL)**. Meperidine, a mu-opioid analgesic, carries a known risk of **seizure provocation** and can also cause **respiratory depression**. In a patient with AML and active cytopenia, the hematologic instability (low platelets) and the drug's potential to precipitate seizures create a dual threat: one from the underlying disease state and the other from the medication's adverse effect profile. Additionally, the patient is already on **oxycodone** and **lorazepam**, both central nervous system depressants, which synergistically increase the risk of respiratory depression and sedation when combined with meperidine.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 370, "uid": "mimic-age-22059910-Scopolamine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants and anticholinergic agents. Specifically, the ordered scopolamine (1 PTCH, once daily) is an anticholinergic with strong CNS depressant properties. The patient is already receiving lorazepam (0.5-2 mg IV), haloperidol (2 mg IV), morphine (2 mg IV), metoprolol (5 mg IV), and ipratropium (NEB). These drugs collectively produce additive sedation, respiratory depression, and anticholinergic burden, which are amplified by scopolamine's anticholinergic and sedative effects. The patient's underlying acute respiratory failure and pulmonary embolism further reduce respiratory reserve, making any additional depressant effect dangerous.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 371, "uid": "mimic-safe-23300884-Lidocaine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's current medication list, specifically the concurrent use of lidocaine with multiple drugs that can alter lidocaine metabolism or increase lidocaine toxicity. The patient is receiving vancomycin, cefepime, metoclopramide, ondansetron, diphenhydramine, hydromorphone, and heparin - all of which are known to inhibit or induce CYP3A4, the primary enzyme responsible for lidocaine clearance. Additionally, the presence of heparin (an anticoagulant) and the patient's postoperative infection status further complicate the safety profile.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
