{"idx": 373, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22987108-Diltiazem", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severe hepatic impairment (cirrhosis, INR 1.5, ALT 354/AST 968) and moderate renal impairment (eGFR 43) create a dual hazard. Diltiazem is hepatically metabolized (CYP3A4) and its clearance is reduced in cirrhosis, leading to elevated systemic exposure and risk of hypotension, bradycardia, and cardiac arrhythmias. Additionally, the patient is on multiple vasopressors (norepinephrine, fentanyl, midodrine) and has hepatorenal syndrome, which further compromises hemodynamic stability. The combination of diltiazem with these agents can produce additive hypotensive effects, and the patient's low platelet count (61 K/uL) raises bleeding risk due to impaired coagulation. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 374, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29839885-Alprazolam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's mechanical complication of an automatic implantable cardiac defibrillator (ICD) combined with chronic systolic heart failure. Alprazolam, a benzodiazepine, can cause dose-dependent respiratory depression and sedation, which in a patient with compromised cardiac function and an ICD may precipitate hemodynamic instability, arrhythmia, or decompensation. Additionally, the patient's moderate renal impairment (eGFR 42) and concurrent use of oxycodone (a mu-opioid) create a high-risk drug-drug interaction for additive CNS and respiratory depression. The presence of multiple cardiovascular comorbidities (congestive heart failure, unspecified CHF, obesity) further amplifies the danger.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 375, "uid": "mimic-safe-20192635-Docusate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile is dominated by acute kidney failure (eGFR 35 mL/min/1.73m2) and a complex polypharmacy regimen. The ordered docusate (100 mg, 2 times/day, PO/NG) introduces a hazard through its potential to alter drug absorption and exacerbate electrolyte disturbances in the setting of renal impairment.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 376, "uid": "mimic-safe-28157142-Tamsulosin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's nonrheumatic aortic stenosis (a fixed valvular obstruction) combined with advanced age (87 years) and moderate renal impairment (eGFR 32 mL/min/1.73m2). Tamsulosin, a selective \u03b11A-adrenergic blocker, is contraindicated in severe aortic stenosis because it can cause reflex tachycardia and vasodilation, potentially precipitating acute decompensation or syncope. Additionally, the patient's acute kidney injury and oliguria raise concern for further renal deterioration if hypotension occurs. The concurrent use of furosemide (loop diuretic) and metoprolol (beta-blocker) further amplifies the risk of hypotension and bradycardia. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 377, "uid": "mimic-ddi-26703331-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute respiratory failure with hypoxia (eGFR 48, age 72, BMI 29). Oxycodone, a mu-opioid agonist, directly depresses the central respiratory drive, which is already compromised. This creates a high risk of worsening hypoxia, hypercapnia, and respiratory arrest. Additionally, the patient is currently receiving morphine, a potent opioid, which produces additive respiratory depression. The combination of two opioids in a patient with acute respiratory failure is contraindicated. The renal impairment (eGFR 48) does not alter oxycodone clearance significantly, but the respiratory risk remains the decisive factor.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 378, "uid": "mimic-safe-27738145-Meropenem", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's **acute respiratory failure with ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP)** and the concurrent use of **fentanyl (opioid analgesic)** and **propofol (sedative)**. The ordered **meropenem** is a broad-spectrum cephalosporin that carries a known risk of **seizure** (especially in patients with renal impairment or those on certain drugs). In this setting, the patient's compromised respiratory status and sedative/analgesic burden already predispose to central nervous system depression and respiratory depression. Adding a seizure-provoking agent in a patient with unstable respiratory function creates a dangerous synergy, increasing the likelihood of adverse events such as status epilepticus, further respiratory compromise, or altered sedation levels that could mask clinical deterioration.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 379, "uid": "mimic-safe-24256866-Ipratropium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute-on-chronic systolic heart failure (eGFR 41, hypernatremia, alkalosis, hypotension) combined with multiple concurrent medications (heparin, alteplase, furosemide, insulin, glucagon, etc.) creates a high-risk milieu. Ipratropium, a bronchodilator, can cause tachycardia and increase heart rate, which may destabilize a compromised heart. Additionally, the patient's thrombocytopenia (platelets 91 K/uL) and acute kidney failure raise concerns about drug accumulation and bleeding risk. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 380, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27617929-Furosemide", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severely reduced eGFR (17 mL/min/1.73m2) and acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis create a critical hazard for furosemide. Furosemide is a loop diuretic that requires adequate renal function to clear its active metabolite; in severe renal impairment, accumulation can precipitate hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, and further renal injury. Additionally, the patient's concurrent use of fentanyl (a potent opioid) and norepinephrine (vasopressor) raises the risk of electrolyte disturbances and cardiovascular instability when diuretic therapy is initiated.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 381, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27411876-Fentanyl", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73m2) and the concurrent use of multiple drugs with known fentanyl interactions create a hazardous scenario. The primary conflict is the co-administration of fentanyl with lidocaine, which can potentiate respiratory depression, and the patient's compromised renal clearance, which may lead to fentanyl accumulation and prolonged sedation. Additionally, the presence of other interacting agents (e.g., propofol, magnesium, calcium gluconate, furosemide, bisacodyl, senna, vancomycin, piperacillin, atorvastatin, quetiapine, nicardipine, acetaminophen, potassium chloride, acetylsalicylic acid) further elevates the risk of additive CNS and respiratory depression, as well as electrolyte disturbances. The patient's encephalopathy and pneumonia also increase vulnerability to respiratory compromise.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 382, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28108313-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of hydralazine (10-20 mg IV) and oxycodone (5-10 mg PO/NG) creates a pharmacodynamic interaction that elevates the risk of hypotension and bradycardia. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (72 years) and pre-existing occlusion and stenosis of the right carotid artery further amplify the hazard of reduced cerebral perfusion, making the combination unsafe.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 383, "uid": "mimic-renal-24698912-Tramadol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 21 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with the ordered tramadol dose of 25 mg. Tramadol is extensively metabolized by CYP2D6 to its active metabolite M1, and its elimination half-life is prolonged in renal failure. Additionally, the patient is on multiple interacting drugs (e.g., metronidazole, vancomycin, amlodipine, atorvastatin, insulin, heparin) that can further alter tramadol pharmacokinetics or pharmacodynamics, increasing the risk of serotonin syndrome, excessive sedation, and respiratory depression. The presence of sepsis, acidosis, and hyponatremia further compromises central nervous system stability, making even a standard dose dangerous.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
