{"idx": 385, "uid": "mimic-age-22130791-Hyoscyamine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's advanced age (88 years), severe renal impairment (eGFR 13 mL/min/1.73m2), and current use of multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants (fentanyl, propofol, hydromorphone, haloperidol, scopolamine, glycopyrronium, ondansetron) create a high-risk profile for hyoscyamine. The drug's anticholinergic properties will further impair CNS function and exacerbate respiratory depression, while its elimination half-life is prolonged in renal failure, increasing the likelihood of toxic accumulation.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 386, "uid": "mimic-age-27505812-Metoclopramide", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple drugs with known QT-prolonging or proarrhythmic properties - including morphine, oxycodone, phenylephrine, nitroglycerin, and metoclopramide - creates a hazard for additive QT interval prolongation and torsades de pointes. Additionally, metoclopramide's central nervous system effects (e.g., extrapyramidal symptoms) are amplified by the presence of propofol and neostigmine, which can alter neuromuscular transmission and sedation. The patient's underlying coronary atherosclerosis and intermediate coronary syndrome further increase vulnerability to arrhythmias and hemodynamic instability. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 387, "uid": "mimic-safe-25172300-Ezetimibe", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 27 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with the concurrent use of multiple renally cleared drugs. Ezetimibe is not renally eliminated, but the patient's polypharmacy includes Torasemide (loop diuretic), Carvedilol (beta-blocker), Irbesartan (ACE inhibitor), Spironolactone (potassium-sparing diuretic), Vancomycin (vancomycin is renally excreted; its clearance is compromised at eGFR <30), and Oxycodone (metabolite renally excreted). The addition of ezetimibe may alter electrolyte balance (e.g., potassium) or interact with diuretics, and the renal impairment magnifies the risk of drug accumulation and toxicity for the co-administered agents. Additionally, the patient's atrial fibrillation, congestive heart failure, and diabetes mellitus create a vulnerable cardiovascular profile that could be exacerbated by any adverse drug effect.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 388, "uid": "mimic-safe-27487226-Fluticasonetopical", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile is dominated by severe renal impairment (eGFR 39 mL/min/1.73m2, acute kidney failure) and a complex polypharmacy regimen. The ordered fluticasone topical spray introduces a drug-drug interaction hazard with fluoxetine, a potent CYP2C9 inhibitor, and also carries a risk of systemic absorption leading to additive electrolyte disturbances (hyperkalemia) in the setting of renal dysfunction and concurrent potassium 5.9 mEq/L. Additionally, the patient's acute-on-chronic diastolic heart failure and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation create a proarrhythmic vulnerability that could be exacerbated by any systemic corticosteroid effect. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions for topical fluticasone, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 389, "uid": "mimic-age-23721604-Paroxetine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (82 years) combined with moderate renal impairment (eGFR 38 mL/min/1.73m2) and a concurrent Clostridium difficile infection. The ordered paroxetine 40 mg daily is a high dose for an elderly patient with compromised renal function, and the infection itself further elevates the risk of CNS adverse effects (e.g., confusion, seizures) and gastrointestinal complications. Additionally, the patient is on multiple interacting medications (buspirone, omeprazole, ceftriaxone, azithromycin, atorvastatin, insulin, glucagon) that can alter paroxetine's pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics, compounding the danger.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 390, "uid": "mimic-age-26226543-Clonazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute-on-chronic diastolic heart failure with acute respiratory failure and hypoxia, compounded by moderate renal impairment (eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and electrolyte-altering medications. Clonazepam, a benzodiazepine, is contraindicated in this setting due to its potent respiratory-depressant and sedative effects, which can worsen hypoxia and precipitate respiratory failure. Additionally, the patient's hypokalemia (K+ 2.9 mEq/L) and concurrent furosemide therapy increase the risk of further electrolyte disturbances, and the presence of myelodysplastic syndrome raises concerns about drug-induced bone marrow suppression. The combination of these factors overrides any general safety assumptions for a low dose of clonazepam.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 391, "uid": "mimic-age-23473524-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's advanced age (81 years) and the concurrent use of multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants. The ordered lorazepam, a benzodiazepine, will add to the cumulative sedative burden from existing medications such as propofol (a potent CNS depressant), insulin, and possibly others. This combination elevates the risk of excessive sedation, respiratory depression, and falls, especially in an elderly patient with acute respiratory failure and heart failure.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 392, "uid": "mimic-safe-28829452-Acetylcysteine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute respiratory failure with hypoxia. Acetylcysteine, when administered via nebulization, can cause bronchospasm, laryngeal edema, and worsening hypoxia in patients with pre-existing respiratory compromise. This directly conflicts with the patient's unstable respiratory status.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 393, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22732862-Prednisolone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of **lorazepam** (a benzodiazepine) and the ordered **prednisolone** (a corticosteroid). The patient's chronic condition of **acute myeloid leukemia** with pancytopenia (low platelets) further amplifies the risk. The specific attribute is the **current medication** lorazepam, which creates a pharmacodynamic interaction with prednisolone.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 394, "uid": "mimic-safe-24717014-Gabapentin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's chronic kidney disease (eGFR 66 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with the prescribed gabapentin dose of 100 mg three times daily. Gabapentin is renally eliminated, and its elimination half-life is prolonged when renal function falls below 60 mL/min. The standard maximum daily dose for mild renal impairment (eGFR 60-30) is 300 mg/day, but the ordered regimen (300 mg/day) exceeds this limit, risking accumulation and neurotoxicity (dizziness, somnolence, ataxia). Additionally, the patient's age (67 years) further reduces renal reserve, compounding the risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 395, "uid": "mimic-safe-26048429-Calciumgluconate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's current medication list, specifically the intravenous administration of Magnesium sulfate (2 gm, IV) and Potassium chloride (60 mEq, IV), combined with the ordered Calcium gluconate (2 g, IV). The core conflict is a drug-drug interaction that can precipitate life-threatening hypercalcemia and hypermagnesemia, especially in a patient with underlying acidosis and esophageal pathology.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 396, "uid": "mimic-safe-29374560-Finasteride", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of **warfarin** (7.5 mg daily) and **heparin** (5000 units SC three times daily) creates a dual anticoagulation hazard. Finasteride, while not directly anticoagulant, can alter warfarin metabolism via CYP3A4 inhibition, potentially elevating INR and increasing bleeding risk. Additionally, the patient's **atrial fibrillation** and **coronary atherosclerosis of autologous vein bypass graft** are prothrombotic conditions; any drug that destabilizes hemostasis or masks bleeding signs is contraindicated. The combination of these factors overrides general safety assumptions, rendering the finasteride order unsafe.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 397, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22508257-Morphine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants - including oxycodone, hydrocodone, lidocaine, trazodone, and senna - combined with the proposed morphine creates a high-risk pharmacodynamic interaction. Additionally, the patient's chronic diastolic heart failure and atrial fibrillation amplify the hazard of opioid-induced respiratory depression and hemodynamic instability. The presence of multiple interacting agents overrides any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 398, "uid": "mimic-safe-24656677-Cyanocobalamin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's uncontrolled diabetes with ketoacidosis, acute kidney failure (eGFR 41), and concurrent use of multiple interacting medications (insulin, atorvastatin, buspirone, glucagon, heparin, ranitidine, omeprazole, paroxetine) create a high-risk scenario for cyanocobalamin administration. The drug's potential to cause reversible optic neuropathy, superimposed on pre-existing metabolic and renal compromise, and the likelihood of altered absorption or toxicity due to polypharmacy and age-related vulnerability, override any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 399, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28477280-Ciprofloxacin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile contains multiple interacting hazards that collectively render ciprofloxacin unsafe. The primary conflicts are: (1) concurrent use of metronidazole and ciprofloxacin, which can cause additive neurotoxicity (e.g., seizures, dizziness); (2) malignant intrahepatic bile duct neoplasm with elevated ALT/AST and INR 1.6, indicating hepatic impairment that may reduce fluoroquinolone clearance and increase hepatotoxicity risk; (3) moderate renal impairment (eGFR 55) that could slow drug elimination, though ciprofloxacin is primarily hepatically cleared; (4) polypharmacy with numerous CNS-active agents (hydromorphone, lorazepam, ondansetron, bupropion) that synergistically depress the central nervous system, raising the risk of respiratory depression and sedation; and (5) septic shock and septicemia, where fluoroquinolone-associated QT prolongation and neuromuscular blockade could worsen hemodynamic instability and complicate sepsis management.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 400, "uid": "mimic-safe-24490144-Ranitidine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple drugs with known QT-prolonging or proarrhythmic potential - including fentanyl, oxycodone, phenylephrine, magnesium sulfate, potassium chloride, and ranitidine - creates a hazard for additive QT interval prolongation and torsades de pointes. Additionally, the patient's obesity (BMI 35.2) and advanced age (62 years) further increase susceptibility to cardiac arrhythmias. The ordered ranitidine dose (150 mg BID) is standard, but the cumulative risk from polypharmacy overrides general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 401, "uid": "mimic-age-24540843-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's advanced age (79 years), moderate renal impairment (eGFR 56 mL/min/1.73m2), and extensive polypharmacy list create a high-risk scenario for lorazepam administration. The specific hazards are advanced age (increased CNS sensitivity), renal impairment (reduced clearance of lorazepam and its active metabolite), and concurrent use of multiple CNS-active and hemodynamic agents (e.g., hydralazine, phenylephrine, furosemide, heparin, insulin, magnesium sulfate, piperacillin, fosphenytoin, atorvastatin, famotidine, salbutamol, ondansetron, senna, docusate, bisacodyl, neutra-phos). These factors collectively amplify the risk of excessive sedation, respiratory depression, hypotension, electrolyte disturbances, and drug-drug interactions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 402, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28909879-Fosphenytoin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the concurrent administration of Fosphenytoin (a prodrug of phenytoin) with Fentanyl, Lorazepam, and Propofol - all potent central nervous system (CNS) depressants. The patient's alcohol withdrawal further amplifies CNS vulnerability. This polypharmacy creates a synergistic risk of excessive sedation, respiratory depression, and loss of airway protection.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 403, "uid": "mimic-ddi-23473524-Piperacillin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"### 1. CLINICAL ASSESSMENT\\nThe patient's advanced age (81 years) and multiple concurrent medications - including levothyroxine, insulin, glucagon, famotidine, heparin, miconazole, vancomycin, lorazepam, and others - create a high-risk profile for drug-drug interactions and altered pharmacokinetics. The ordered piperacillin dose (4.5 g IV) is standard for pseudomonal sepsis, but the patient's frailty and polypharmacy override general safety assumptions.\\n\\n### 2. PHARMACOLOGICAL BASIS\\nPiperacillin is a broad-spectrum beta-lactam antibiotic. It can displace warfarin (not present here) and other drugs from protein-binding sites, and its renal clearance may be affected by concurrent nephrotoxic agents. Additionally, piperacillin's neurotoxicity (e.g., seizures) is dose-dependent, and elderly patients have reduced hepatic and renal clearance, increasing the risk of accumulation and adverse effects.\\n\\n### 3. PATIENT-SPECIFIC ANALYSIS\\nWhile piperacillin is appropriate for pseudomonal sepsis, the patient's age and polypharmacy introduce a net hazard that outweighs the benefit. The combination of advanced age, normal renal function (eGFR 107) does not mitigate the risk of drug interactions with her existing regimen (e.g., heparin, lorazepam, vancomycin). General safety assumptions (e.g., \\\"normal renal function is safe\\\") are overridden by the specific profile, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 404, "uid": "mimic-safe-28166872-Nystatin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - fentanyl, midazolam, propofol, lorazepam, morphine, hydralazine, levetiracetam, phenylephrine, and ampicillin - collectively create a high-risk pharmacodynamic profile. Nystatin, though generally safe, can cause gastrointestinal irritation and diarrhea, which in this setting may precipitate or worsen opioid-induced constipation, respiratory compromise, and electrolyte disturbances (notably hypokalemia, as evidenced by the potassium of 3.1 mEq/L). The patient's acute respiratory failure and methicillin-resistant pneumonia further amplify vulnerability to adverse events. Thus, the ordered nystatin is unsafe due to the additive risks of altered bowel function and potential electrolyte shifts in a critically ill patient.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 405, "uid": "mimic-age-29496232-Ketorolac", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk", "Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent use of ketorolac (a non-selective NSAID) with citalopram (a serotonergic antidepressant) in a patient with advanced age (76 years), mild renal impairment (eGFR 70), and a history of atrial fibrillation. This combination creates a high-risk scenario for serotonin syndrome and exacerbates bleeding risk due to additive antiplatelet/anticoagulant effects.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 406, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22196214-Morphine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent administration of morphine with hydromorphone, a full mu-opioid agonist, creating a dangerous additive opioid effect. Additionally, the patient's current use of diazepam (a benzodiazepine) further amplifies CNS depression, and the presence of pleural effusion with possible respiratory compromise heightens the risk of respiratory depression. The patient's obesity (BMI 32.9) may contribute to altered pharmacokinetics, but the immediate conflict is the drug-drug interaction.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 407, "uid": "mimic-renal-27417763-Tramadol", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk", "Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 26 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (86 years) create a high-risk scenario for tramadol accumulation and CNS depression. Additionally, the concurrent use of multiple CNS-active drugs (morphine, hydralazine, ciprofloxacin) and the presence of hyperosmolality/hypernatremia further compound the hazard.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 408, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24547356-Ciprofloxacin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's current use of **heparin** (5000 UNIT, 2 times/day, SC) and **hydromorphone** (20 mg, IVPCA) in combination with the ordered **ciprofloxacin** (2 drop, 2 times/day, left ear). The specific attributes are: (1) **heparin** is a potent anticoagulant, and (2) **hydromorphone** is a strong opioid analgesic. Ciprofloxacin is a moderate CYP3A4 inhibitor, and it also carries a known risk of exacerbating neuromuscular blockade when co-administered with neuromuscular blocking agents. The patient's profile - including the active anticoagulant and opioid - creates a dual, additive risk that overrides any general safety assumptions.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 409, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29839885-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's mechanical complication of an automatic implantable cardiac defibrillator (ICD) combined with moderate renal impairment (eGFR 42 mL/min/1.73m2). Oxycodone, a mu-opioid agonist, can cause dose-dependent respiratory depression and, via its mu-receptor agonism, may provoke bradycardia, hypotension, and QT-interval prolongation. In a patient with an ICD, any hemodynamic instability or arrhythmia can trigger inappropriate ICD discharge. Additionally, oxycodone is primarily renally excreted as active metabolites (noroxycodone), and reduced renal clearance in this patient increases the risk of accumulation and toxicity. The concurrent use of alprazolam (a benzodiazepine) further amplifies CNS and respiratory depression, creating a dangerous additive effect. The patient's chronic systolic heart failure and congestive heart failure exacerbate the vulnerability to opioid-induced hypotension and fluid retention.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 410, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27089790-Morphine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple central nervous system depressants (propofol, metoclopramide, oxycodone, morphine) and electrolyte-altering agents (magnesium sulfate, potassium chloride, calcium gluconate) creates a high-risk pharmacodynamic and pharmacokinetic interaction profile. The addition of morphine to this polypharmacy list amplifies the potential for additive respiratory depression, excessive sedation, and cardiac arrhythmias, especially in a 78-year-old with coronary atherosclerosis and mild renal impairment.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 411, "uid": "mimic-safe-26529390-Tiotropium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with the ordered tiotropium dose. Tiotropium is primarily eliminated unchanged via the kidneys; in acute renal impairment, drug accumulation can occur, increasing the risk of excessive anticholinergic effects (e.g., tachycardia, confusion, constipation). Additionally, the patient's age (72 years) further reduces renal reserve, compounding the risk. The presence of multiple concurrent medications (glucagon, tramadol, escitalopram, etc.) does not directly interact with tiotropium, but the renal impairment is the decisive factor.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 412, "uid": "mimic-safe-23473524-Metronidazole", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's advanced age (81 years) and the concurrent use of metronidazole with multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants and other agents that independently depress the CNS. The specific drug-drug interactions and age-related vulnerability create a cumulative risk of excessive sedation, respiratory depression, and altered mental status.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 413, "uid": "mimic-age-27417763-Scopolamine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are advanced age (86 years), moderate renal impairment (eGFR 32 mL/min), concurrent use of morphine and acetaminophen, and the presence of hyperosmolality/hypernatremia. Scopolamine, an anticholinergic agent, is contraindicated in this profile because it can precipitate delirium, worsen hyperosmolality, and cause severe anticholinergic toxicity (e.g., tachycardia, confusion, hallucinations). The patient's renal impairment reduces clearance of scopolamine metabolites, and the opioid coadministration amplifies CNS depression and respiratory depression risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 414, "uid": "mimic-ddi-29842315-Methadone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 6 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (89 years) create a critical hazard when methadone is administered. Methadone is extensively renally excreted as active metabolites (e.g., methadone N-oxide), and in severe renal impairment, clearance is markedly reduced, leading to drug accumulation and prolonged QTc interval. This, combined with pre-existing cardiac arrest risk from NSTEMI and diastolic heart failure, elevates the likelihood of torsades de pointes. Additionally, the patient's respiratory failure and COPD further amplify the risk of methadone-induced respiratory depression, especially at a dose of 20 mg daily. The concurrent gabapentin use adds to CNS depression and respiratory compromise. All these factors override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 415, "uid": "mimic-renal-22413744-Magnesiumoxide", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 13 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of magnesium sulfate (2 gm IV daily) create a hazard when adding magnesium oxide. Magnesium oxide is a poorly absorbed oral antacid, but in the setting of severe renal impairment, even small additional magnesium loads can precipitate hypermagnesemia, which is particularly dangerous when combined with intravenous magnesium. The patient's current magnesium sulfate infusion provides a baseline magnesium load; adding oral magnesium oxide risks cumulative toxicity, especially given the reduced renal clearance.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 416, "uid": "mimic-ddi-20285402-Lorazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of **hydromorphone** (6-8 mg PO/NG) and the newly ordered **lorazepam** (1 mg PO/NG). Both are central nervous system (CNS) depressants, and their co-administration creates a pharmacodynamic interaction that can lead to excessive sedation, respiratory depression, and impaired consciousness.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 417, "uid": "mimic-safe-24745425-Potassiumchloride", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of **meperidine (12.5 mg IV)** and **fentanyl (12.5-50 mcg IV)**, both potent opioid analgesics, combined with the administration of **potassium chloride 20 mEq IV**. The patient's underlying **chronic systolic heart failure (congestive)** and **supraventricular tachycardia** further amplify the risk.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 418, "uid": "mimic-safe-25933959-Hydralazine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of **glucagon (1 mg IM)** and **insulin human (SC)** creates a direct pharmacodynamic conflict with hydralazine. Glucagon elevates blood glucose by promoting hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, while insulin lowers glucose. Hydralazine, a direct vasodilator, can cause reflex tachycardia and increased sympathetic outflow, which may antagonize the hypoglycemic effect of insulin and blunt the hyperglycemic response to glucagon. This interaction destabilizes glycemic control, especially in a patient with type 2 diabetes and foot ulcer who requires tight glucose management to prevent infection and wound complications.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 419, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27738145-Ondansetron", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - fentanyl, propofol, levofloxacin, hydromorphone, and sertraline - all carry known QT-prolonging potential. Adding ondansetron, which is a potent hERG potassium channel blocker, creates a cumulative risk of life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias, particularly torsades de pointes. The patient's underlying malignant neoplasm of the pancreas and secondary liver cancer may further predispose to electrolyte disturbances and metabolic instability, compounding the hazard.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 420, "uid": "mimic-ddi-28613200-Oxycodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medication list includes morphine (2-4 mg IV) and oxycodone (5 mg PO/NG). Both are mu-opioid agonists. Co-administration produces additive central nervous system and respiratory depression, increasing the risk of hypoventilation, sedation, and overdose. Additionally, the patient is on diphenhydramine (25 mg PO/NG), a potent antihistamine with sedative properties, which further amplifies CNS depression. The presence of multiple CNS-active agents (morphine, oxycodone, diphenhydramine) creates a synergistic hazard that overrides any general safety assumptions for oxycodone alone.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 421, "uid": "mimic-safe-21540783-Ciprofloxacin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the concurrent use of ciprofloxacin with metoprolol, heparin, and the patient's atrial fibrillation. Ciprofloxacin is a moderate CYP1A2 inhibitor, which can elevate metoprolol concentrations, increasing the risk of bradycardia and hypotension. Additionally, ciprofloxacin may potentiate the anticoagulant effect of heparin, raising the bleeding risk. The patient's atrial fibrillation further amplifies the cardiac concern, as any rate-slowing effect from elevated metoprolol could destabilize rhythm control. The mild renal impairment (eGFR 72) does not significantly alter ciprofloxacin clearance, but the drug-drug and drug-disease interactions override general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 422, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22950920-Morphine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the concurrent administration of morphine with oxycodone, hydromorphone, and diazepam. The patient is already receiving three potent opioid agonists (oxycodone 5 mg PO/NG, hydromorphone 0.25 mg IV, and morphine 30 mg PO) plus a benzodiazepine (diazepam 5 mg PO/NG). This polypharmacy creates a high-risk additive central nervous system (CNS) depression and respiratory depression scenario.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 423, "uid": "mimic-safe-27993466-Simethicone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of multiple anticoagulants (heparin, warfarin, and digoxin) and the ordered simethicone. Simethicone, though generally considered safe, can alter gastric pH and potentially affect the absorption of certain medications, including warfarin and digoxin. In a patient with multiple interacting drugs and a history of pulmonary embolism, even minor absorption changes may increase bleeding risk or digoxin toxicity. The patient's age (78 years) further amplifies susceptibility to drug interactions and adverse effects.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 424, "uid": "mimic-safe-22205327-Calciumgluconate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis (eGFR 46 mL/min/1.73m2) creates a critical hazard for intravenous calcium gluconate. This drug can precipitate hypocalcemia, hypercalcemia, or severe hypotension, and in the setting of renal impairment, calcium clearance is reduced, increasing the risk of toxicity. Additionally, the patient's long QT syndrome is a direct contraindication to calcium gluconate, as calcium can prolong the QT interval and trigger torsades de pointes. The concurrent use of epinephrine and phenylephrine further elevates the risk of arrhythmia and hemodynamic instability. These overlapping vulnerabilities override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 425, "uid": "mimic-ddi-21791856-Bupropion", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile contains multiple interacting hazards that collectively render bupropion unsafe. The primary conflicts are: (1) **acute kidney failure (eGFR 14 mL/min/1.73m2)**, which impairs drug clearance and increases systemic exposure; (2) **respiratory failure with hypoxia** from COPD exacerbation, where bupropion's dose-dependent stimulation of the central nervous system can worsen respiratory drive; (3) **concurrent use of multiple CNS-active medications** (lorazepam, methylprednisolone, metolazone, carvedilol, insulin, torasemide, levothyroxine, ondansetron, ezetimibe, febuxostat, glucagon, ipratropium, acetylsalicylic acid) that synergistically depress the respiratory center; and (4) **severe obesity (BMI 32.2)**, which independently elevates the risk of respiratory compromise and may alter bupropion pharmacokinetics. These factors override any general safety assumptions about bupropion monotherapy.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
