{"idx": 427, "uid": "mimic-ddi-21027282-Warfarin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severely reduced renal function (eGFR 23 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of multiple interacting drugs (heparin, amiodarone, furosemide, famotidine, calcium gluconate, potassium chloride, magnesium sulfate) create a high-risk scenario for warfarin therapy. The combination of advanced age (79 years) and septicemia further amplifies bleeding vulnerability. The ordered warfarin dose (2 mg) is standard, but the patient's profile overrides general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": true,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 428, "uid": "mimic-safe-23831430-Vancomycin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's **acute kidney failure (unspecified)** combined with the ordered **vancomycin** dose of 1000 mg three times daily. This regimen exceeds the standard loading dose for a patient with normal renal function, and the presence of acute kidney failure creates a high risk of drug accumulation and toxicity.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 429, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22390287-Ondansetron", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the concurrent use of citalopram (10 mg daily) and ondansetron (8 mg IV). Both agents are known to prolong the QT interval via inhibition of the hERG potassium channel. The patient's age (75 years) further amplifies this risk, as elderly individuals have reduced cardiac repolarization reserve and higher baseline QTc variability. This combination creates a synergistic proarrhythmic effect, elevating the probability of torsades de pointes, especially in the setting of atrial fibrillation (a pre-existing cardiac condition).\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 430, "uid": "mimic-age-29276678-Scopolamine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute respiratory failure with hypoxia, combined with a polypharmacy regimen including multiple CNS depressants (lorazepam, hydromorphone, quetiapine, olanzapine, glycopyrronium, amiodarone) and a history of thrombocytopenia (platelets 27 K/uL), creates a high-risk scenario for scopolamine. Scopolamine is a potent anticholinergic agent that can cause central and peripheral anticholinergic toxicity, including respiratory depression, tachycardia, and delirium. In a patient with pre-existing hypoxia and multiple respiratory depressants, adding scopolamine would likely worsen respiratory function and increase the risk of adverse events. Additionally, the patient's thrombocytopenia raises the concern for bleeding complications, as scopolamine can impair platelet aggregation and wound healing. The concurrent use of amiodarone (a QT-prolonging agent) with scopolamine further elevates the risk of arrhythmia. All these factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 431, "uid": "mimic-ddi-25239799-Glycopyrronium", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple high-risk medications - including fentanyl, oxycodone, nitroglycerin, phenylephrine, metoclopramide, citalopram, and tramadol - creates a profound hazard when combined with glycopyrronium. Glycopyrronium is a long-acting anticholinergic that can blunt the cardiovascular and respiratory depressant effects of opioids, leading to masked overdose and respiratory compromise. Additionally, the patient's atrial fibrillation, coronary atherosclerosis, and Mobitz II block are exacerbated by glycopyrronium's potential to increase heart rate and reduce vagal tone, risking arrhythmia or hemodynamic instability. The high number of interacting drugs and underlying cardiac vulnerability override any general safety assumptions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": true,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 432, "uid": "mimic-safe-29780751-Rivaroxaban", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of Sucralfate, a known potent inhibitor of rivaroxaban absorption, creates a direct drug-drug interaction hazard. Additionally, the patient's platelet count of 89 \u00d7 10^9/L indicates pre-existing thrombocytopenia, which synergistically elevates bleeding risk. These two factors - a drug-drug interaction that reduces rivaroxaban efficacy and a baseline hematologic abnormality - override any general safety assumptions, rendering the order unsafe.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 433, "uid": "mimic-safe-22342963-Potassiumchloride", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of **insulin human (IV drip)** and **glucagon (IM)**, both of which directly alter potassium homeostasis. Potassium chloride (20 mEq IV) is a potassium-repletion agent, and its administration in this setting creates a high risk of **hyperkalemia**.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
