{"idx": 448, "uid": "mimic-age-23112364-Nifedipine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Age Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's end-stage renal disease (eGFR 10 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent use of multiple nephrotoxic agents (Senna, Docusate, Nephrocaps, Ondansetron, Bisacodyl, Heparin, Torasemide) create a high-risk scenario. Nifedipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, is primarily renally eliminated; in severe renal impairment, its half-life is prolonged, increasing the likelihood of accumulation and toxicity (hypotension, reflex tachycardia, peripheral edema). Additionally, the patient's hyperpotassemia and noncompliance history further amplify the danger of electrolyte disturbances and adverse drug reactions.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 449, "uid": "mimic-safe-20214994-Metronidazole", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's current medication list, particularly the intravenous magnesium sulfate (2 g) and potassium chloride (40 mEq). Metronidazole, when administered intravenously, can cause dose-dependent hypotension and, in the presence of electrolyte disturbances, may precipitate or worsen hypotensive episodes. The patient's acute kidney failure with tubular necrosis further compromises renal reserve, reducing the ability to clear metronidazole metabolites and increasing the risk of accumulation and neurotoxicity. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (75 years) and multiple comorbidities (atrial fibrillation, congestive heart failure, acute respiratory failure, systemic inflammatory response syndrome) amplify susceptibility to adverse drug reactions.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 450, "uid": "mimic-safe-21216581-Potassiumchloride", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of apixaban (a direct factor Xa inhibitor) and the ordered potassium chloride. Apixaban carries a black-box warning for serious gastrointestinal bleeding, and potassium chloride is a known irritant that can cause mucosal injury. The combination elevates the risk of severe, potentially life-threatening GI hemorrhage. Additionally, the patient's atrial flutter and history of TIA/cerebral infarction without residual deficits indicate a prothrombotic state, but the primary conflict is the drug-drug interaction.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 451, "uid": "mimic-ddi-23819016-Diazepam", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of lorazepam (1 mg daily) and the ordered diazepam (10 mg IV) creates a dual hazard: additive CNS depression and a potential pharmacokinetic interaction. Additionally, the patient's moderate renal impairment (eGFR 32 mL/min) and hepatic impairment (alcoholic hepatitis, elevated ALT/AST) further amplify the risk of diazepam accumulation and prolonged sedation.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 452, "uid": "mimic-safe-21759936-Benzonatate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple central nervous system (CNS) depressants - citalopram, lamotrigine, lidocaine, and ondansetron - combined with the proposed benzonatate creates a hazard of additive CNS depression. Additionally, the presence of acute respiratory failure with hypoxia and pneumonia further amplifies the risk of respiratory depression from benzonatate's cough-suppressant effect.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 453, "uid": "mimic-safe-20297618-Vancomycin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - particularly oxycodone, fentanyl, nitroglycerin, and ranitidine - create a hazard when combined with vancomycin. The primary conflict is the pharmacodynamic interaction between vancomycin and the opioid analgesics, which can potentiate central nervous system depression and respiratory depression. Additionally, vancomycin's known effect on histamine release may exacerbate the hypotensive state induced by nitroglycerin and phenylephrine, while ranitidine's histamine H2 antagonism could theoretically reduce the risk of vancomycin-induced histamine-mediated adverse effects, but the overall profile remains unsafe due to the high-risk polypharmacy.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 454, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22841357-Raltegravir", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's hepatic coma due to unspecified viral hepatitis C with cirrhosis. Raltegravir, a protease inhibitor, undergoes extensive hepatic metabolism (CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, CYP2D6, and CYP1A2). In hepatic coma, liver function is severely compromised, leading to reduced clearance and elevated drug concentrations. This increases the risk of dose-dependent adverse effects such as neutropenia, lactic acidosis, and hepatotoxicity. Additionally, the patient's thrombocytopenia and hyponatremia further complicate management, as raltegravir may exacerbate electrolyte disturbances and bleeding risk.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 455, "uid": "mimic-safe-21027282-Calciumgluconate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 11 mL/min/1.73m2) and concurrent administration of multiple drugs that alter calcium homeostasis create a high-risk scenario for calcium gluconate overdose. The ordered dose of 2 g IV is standard for acute hypocalcemia, but in this setting the patient's compromised kidney function and polypharmacy override general safety assumptions.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
