{"idx": 465, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24982426-Clopidogrel", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of omeprazole (a potent CYP2C19 inhibitor) with the recommended clopidogrel loading dose (300 mg) creates a critical drug-drug interaction hazard. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (81 years) and moderate renal impairment (eGFR 55) further amplify the risk of excessive bleeding.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 466, "uid": "mimic-safe-22342963-Calciumgluconate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's concurrent use of **potassium chloride (20 mEq IV)** and the newly ordered **calcium gluconate (2 g IV)**. Both agents are intravenous electrolyte supplements, and their co-administration creates a direct pharmacodynamic interaction.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 467, "uid": "mimic-renal-24928679-Baclofen", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Renal Impairment Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazards are the patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 6 mL/min/1.73m2, hepatorenal syndrome) and the concurrent use of baclofen with multiple CNS-active medications (diazepam, midodrine, octreotide, levothyroxine, pantoprazole, allopurinol, acetaminophen). These factors together create a high-risk scenario for baclofen accumulation and excessive sedation.\\n\\n2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 468, "uid": "mimic-safe-25239799-Docusate", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile reveals multiple interacting hazards. First, the concurrent use of acetylsalicylic acid (ASA) and docusate creates a bleeding risk: docusate can reduce gastrointestinal mucosal integrity, potentially exacerbating ASA's antiplatelet effect. Second, the patient's atrial fibrillation and coronary atherosclerosis, combined with a history of percutaneous coronary intervention, place him at high risk for thromboembolic events; docusate's laxative action may provoke electrolyte shifts (e.g., hypokalemia) that destabilize cardiac rhythm. Third, the mild renal impairment (eGFR 77) and advanced age (78 years) increase susceptibility to docusate's systemic absorption and adverse effects, such as pruritus or electrolyte disturbances. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 469, "uid": "mimic-ddi-22130791-Erythromycin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's severe renal impairment (eGFR 19 mL/min/1.73m2) and advanced age (88 years) create a high-risk scenario for erythromycin accumulation. Erythromycin is predominantly eliminated renally, and its half-life is prolonged in renal failure. The concurrent use of fentanyl (a potent opioid) and vasopressin (which can cause vasoconstriction and further reduce renal perfusion) amplifies the risk of additive QT prolongation and hypotension. Additionally, the patient's hypovolemic shock and acute respiratory failure with hypoxia mean that any drug-induced cardiovascular or respiratory depression is particularly dangerous. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 470, "uid": "mimic-safe-26529390-Rasburicase", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's acute kidney failure (eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73m2) combined with the ordered rasburicase dose of 6 mg IV. This dose exceeds the recommended maximum for renal impairment, risking precipitation of urate crystals and acute kidney injury exacerbation. Additionally, the patient's splenic infarction (splenic atrophy) may impair drug clearance, further elevating systemic exposure. The concurrent use of tramadol (CYP2D6/3A4 substrate) and escitalopram (CYP2C19/3A4) with rasburicase (CYP1A2/3A4) could theoretically increase tramadol levels, though the interaction is not clinically significant at these doses. The acute respiratory failure with hypoxia and congestive heart failure also raise concern for fluid overload from rasburicase's diuretic effect, but the immediate safety issue is the renal dose toxicity.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 471, "uid": "mimic-safe-26369609-Iron", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's concurrent use of warfarin (3 mg daily) and the ordered iron supplement. Iron is a known inhibitor of warfarin metabolism via CYP2C9, which can elevate the international normalized ratio (INR) and increase bleeding risk. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (91 years), moderate renal impairment (eGFR 53 mL/min), and multiple anticoagulant/antiplatelet agents (warfarin, acetylsalicylic acid, heparin) further amplify the bleeding hazard. The presence of acute cor pulmonale and congestive heart failure adds hemodynamic instability, making any additional pro-hemorrhagic effect dangerous.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 472, "uid": "mimic-ddi-27487226-Colchicine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's profile contains multiple interacting hazards that collectively render colchicine unsafe. The primary drivers are (1) acute kidney failure (eGFR 39 mL/min/1.73m2) and (2) a complex polypharmacy regimen with numerous drugs that either inhibit or induce CYP3A4, the major metabolic pathway for colchicine. Additionally, the patient's severe obesity (BMI 35.8) and underlying cardiac conditions (congestive heart failure, atrial fibrillation, tamponade) further amplify the risk of colchicine toxicity.\\n\\n### 2.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 473, "uid": "mimic-safe-28108313-Dextranlowmolecularweight", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's concurrent use of multiple vasopressors (phenylephrine, hydralazine) and opioids (oxycodone, morphine) with the proposed dextran infusion creates a hazard. Dextran 40 is a low-molecular-weight dextran that can cause hypotension, and when combined with agents that alter vascular tone or blood pressure, it may precipitate severe hypotensive episodes or hemodynamic instability. Additionally, the patient's advanced age (72 years) and pre-existing carotid stenosis increase susceptibility to cerebral hypoperfusion, further elevating the risk of stroke or transient ischemic attack.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 474, "uid": "mimic-safe-22595853-Nicotine", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's portal hypertension with a platelet count of 71 \u00d7 10^9/L, combined with concurrent heparin therapy. Nicotine, a vasoconstrictor, can further impair hepatic blood flow and platelet function, directly antagonizing the anticoagulant effect of heparin and increasing bleeding risk. Additionally, nicotine's metabolism via CYP1A2 may alter the pharmacokinetics of other drugs (e.g., spironolactone, salbutamol) and its prothrombotic effects are particularly dangerous in a cirrhotic liver.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 475, "uid": "mimic-safe-25129047-Heparin", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's current medications - bisacodyl, insulin, glucagon, ondansetron, hydralazine, fentanyl, magnesium sulfate, levetiracetam, nicotine, levofloxacin, calcium gluconate, ipratropium, nicardipine, lorazepam, clobetasol, potassium chloride, clonidine, metoprolol, and famotidine - collectively create a high-risk profile for heparin administration. The primary hazard is the co-administration of heparin with multiple drugs that either directly or indirectly affect hemostasis, leading to a synergistic bleeding risk. Additionally, the patient's cerebral laceration and contusion with loss of consciousness duration unspecified (without open intracranial wound) further elevate the risk of intracranial hemorrhage, which is exacerbated by anticoagulation.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": true,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 476, "uid": "mimic-safe-23473524-Meropenem", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The hazard arises from the patient's advanced age (81 years) and the ordered meropenem dose of 1000 mg once daily. Despite normal renal function (eGFR 107), elderly patients often require dose reduction due to age-related pharmacokinetic changes (e.g., reduced glomerular filtration, altered volume of distribution). The standard adult dose for pseudomonal sepsis is 750-1000 mg every 8 hours; a single daily dose is subtherapeutic and may lead to inadequate pathogen coverage, treatment failure, and increased risk of resistant organisms. Additionally, the patient's acute-on-chronic heart failure and respiratory failure create a vulnerable state where any suboptimal antibiotic regimen can worsen clinical outcomes.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": true,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 477, "uid": "mimic-safe-23473524-Miconazole", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The primary hazard is the patient's advanced age (81 years) combined with the concurrent use of multiple potent CYP3A4 and P-gp inhibitors (famotidine, ciprofloxacin, norepinephrine, propofol, cefepime, heparin, docusate) and the ordered miconazole dose. Miconazole is a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor and P-gp substrate; its co-administration with these agents elevates the risk of drug-drug interactions, leading to supratherapeutic concentrations of the interacting drugs and potential toxicity. Additionally, miconazole's known QT-prolonging effect is amplified in an elderly patient with acute-on-chronic heart failure, increasing the risk of arrhythmia. The patient's septicemic state and respiratory failure further compromise hemodynamic stability, making any added cardiac risk unacceptable.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 478, "uid": "mimic-ddi-24225421-Trazodone", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": false, "gold_categories": ["Drug-Drug Interaction Risk"], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's advanced age (81 years), moderate renal impairment (eGFR 41 mL/min/1.73m2), and extensive polypharmacy list create a high-risk scenario for trazodone administration. The specific hazards are: (1) age-related pharmacodynamic sensitivity (increased sedation, orthostatic hypotension, QT prolongation) and reduced drug clearance; (2) renal impairment, which may impair elimination of trazodone's active metabolite m-CPP; (3) numerous interacting drugs (e.g., buspirone, paroxetine, propranolol, ranitidine, ciprofloxacin, cefepime, insulin, heparin) that can alter trazodone metabolism (CYP3A4, CYP2D6) or additive CNS/sedative effects; and (4) the presence of hyponatremia and hypovolemia, which further heighten the risk of orthostatic hypotension and falls. These factors collectively override any general safety assumptions, necessitating the \\\"Unsafe\\\" verdict.\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": false,\n  \"Age Risk\": true\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
{"idx": 479, "uid": "mimic-safe-27488741-Glucagon", "model": "/users/PCS0289/sarakhosravi/Guardrail/context_unrolling/outputs/ontop_8b/sft+1ep_unrolled_v4pro/final_adapter", "gold_safe": true, "gold_categories": [], "completion": "{\n \"reasoning\": \"The patient's acute-on-chronic diastolic heart failure, chronic passive congestion of the liver, and extensive polypharmacy create a high-risk scenario for glucagon administration. Glucagon elevates blood glucose, increases heart rate, and can precipitate tachyarrhythmias or decompensate heart failure. Additionally, the patient's severe obesity (BMI 45.2) may alter glucagon pharmacokinetics and increase the risk of adverse effects. The concurrent use of multiple drugs (e.g., furosemide, morphine, quetiapine, clindamycin) further complicates safety.\\n\\n###\",\n \"risk_analysis\": {\n  \"Allergy & Adverse Drug Reaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Drug-Drug Interaction Risk\": true,\n  \"Drug-Food Interaction Risk\": false,\n  \"Dosage & Toxicity Risk\": false,\n  \"Renal Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Hepatic Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Cardiac Impairment Risk\": true,\n  \"Respiratory Impairment Risk\": false,\n  \"Bleeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Infection Risk\": false,\n  \"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding Risk\": false,\n  \"Alcohol Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Tobacco Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Substance Use Risk\": false,\n  \"Caffeine Intake Risk\": false,\n  \"Weight/BMI Risk\": true,\n  \"Age Risk\": false\n },\n \"is_safe\": false\n}"}
